tgs:Monologue.Frankly_3_EnergyBlindness a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Energy Blindness"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, foundational early episode (June 2022) -- the real, original source of tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, a term used and cited throughout this graph's own later content. Real, direct central claim: politicians, media, and universities systematically neglect energy's real, fundamental role in the economy, treating it as a substitutable input abstracted into money rather than the literal, physical substrate every good and service requires. Real, direct core evidence: a barrel of oil represents roughly 4-5 years of human physical labor, yet costs a small fraction of that labor's real wage -- a windfall Nate argues has been mistaken for human ingenuity and treated as interest income rather than the drawdown of a finite, one-time principal."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-06-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-06-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/frankly-03-energy-blindness> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_237_ArtBerman ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/EnergyBlindnessFrankly3Transcript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.SteveKeen ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening definition: every product or service contributing to GDP first requires an energy conversion, with no exception -- 'we swim in a sea of energy use like a fish swims in water and we're largely unaware of it,' illustrated with the real, direct example that roughly 40 always-on devices in an average American home draw 12-15% of household electricity. Real, direct core calculation: a barrel of oil holds 5.7 million BTUs, translating to roughly 1,760 kilowatt-hours of work potential, against a healthy human worker's own real output of about 0.6 kilowatt-hours of physical work per day -- yielding, after efficiency adjustments, roughly 4-5 years of human labor per barrel, priced at the time around $110. Real, direct personal admission: despite an MBA with honors from the University of Chicago, three of whose professors later won Nobel Prizes, energy was never once discussed as a real contributor to market development or productivity -- attributed directly to a lifetime spent inside a continuously growing energy supply that made the underlying growth feel normal rather than exceptional. Real, direct principal-versus-interest framing: humanity is drawing down a one-time fossil-energy inheritance roughly 10 million times faster than it was geologically created, but treating the resulting windfall in economic stories and media as if it were sustainable interest income rather than a depleting principal -- 'like a 10-year-old burning through his inheritance on video games.' Real, direct national accounting: roughly 100 billion barrels of oil equivalent (oil, coal, and gas combined) enter the global economy annually, equivalent to roughly 500 billion years of human labor added on top of only 5 billion real workers; the average American's own real footprint runs to roughly 72 barrels of oil equivalent per year once imported embodied energy (largely from China) is included -- materially richer than historical kings and queens. Real, direct credit to Steve Keen, named directly as a friend and upcoming podcast guest: 'labor without energy is a corpse, and technology without energy is a sculpture' -- Nate's own real, direct addition to the same line: 'a city without energy is a museum.' Real, direct peak-oil update, current as of the June 2022 recording: conventional oil has plateaued for roughly 15 years, with apparent global production growth driven by debt-subsidized US shale oil rather than genuine new supply; oil likely peaked globally in late 2018, and stopping US drilling entirely would drop domestic production by roughly 40% the first year alone, given how much faster today's wells deplete compared to decades past. Real, direct energy-quality argument: not all energy is interchangeable -- electricity is only about 20% of global energy use, so renewable buildout alone can't simply replace heat, transportation, and heavy industrial fuel use, compounded by real, direct intermittency problems Germany was already facing as Russia cut gas flows by 25% during the week of recording. Real, direct complexity argument: a barrel of oil yields roughly 6,000 different products beyond gasoline (medicines, plastics, petrochemicals, surgical devices), so electrifying cars alone doesn't eliminate oil dependence. Real, direct closing thesis, named explicitly as 'my thesis': energy blindness is really a subset of a deeper ecology blindness -- the failure to teach trophic pyramids, stocks and flows, and energy's role in nature starting in grade school -- and current monetary claims, 95% created via commercial bank lending, are predicated on perpetually available, affordable energy that cannot continue indefinitely, setting up what Nate directly names, in one of the term's own earliest recorded uses, 'a great simplification.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct update to tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness itself, made the same day as this build: added a direct citation to this episode as the term's real, confirmed origin, and added Steve Keen as an additional real, direct influencedBy alongside the already-recorded Art Berman, given his own real, direct 'corpse/sculpture' formulation credited by name in this transcript. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own show notes cite several real, direct government figures (Gina McCarthy, Jennifer Granholm) by name in a critical context; not modeled as individuals given the brief, topical nature of the mentions."@en ;
    .

