tgs:Monologue.Frankly_11_InSpiteOf a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "In Spite Of..."@en ;
    skos:definition "Prompted directly by real, live nuclear-war rhetoric in the news that week, Nate lays out a real, structured evolutionary-biology framework for understanding spite — behavior that incurs a real cost to oneself, provided it imposes a greater cost on a competitor — as a genuine, evolutionarily adaptive strategy under conditions of relative rather than absolute fitness. Distinguishes relative fitness (reproductive success measured against competitors, the real currency of evolution) from absolute fitness (raw offspring count or physical condition), and situates spite as the fourth quadrant of a real behavioral matrix alongside selfishness, cooperation, and altruism."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-10-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/11-in-spite-of> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.RelativeFitnessSpiteFramework, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct historical argument connects the four behavioral quadrants to economic history: for most of human history, under flat economic stability, cooperation was the dominant strategy within small tribal groups; the past two centuries of real economic growth made selfishness dominant instead, since energy surplus meant individuals no longer needed their tribe to get ahead. Nate's own real, direct concern closes the argument: as growth flattens or reverses, spite becomes a real, live behavioral option whenever people perceive they can no longer get ahead through either selfish individual effort or cooperation — framed with a real, concrete micro-scale example (an economically disadvantaged person throwing rocks at a wealthy neighbor's solar panels) before scaling directly to nuclear war as, in Nate's own real words, 'the big daddy of spite.' He's direct that the same evolutionary wiring that makes a spider in one's hair feel more urgent than a car crash statistic also makes the immediate, visceral logic of striking a rival nation outweigh full recognition of the real, catastrophic consequences for everyone, including the initiating nation itself. Closes with real, direct self-awareness about the show's own limits: 'I am more an expert of the challenges and the constraints we have than the solutions,' offering understanding motivated reasoning and confirmation bias as the real, best available first step toward a genuinely prosocial response, rather than a solved answer."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest provenance note: no formal transcript or show-notes YAML was ever produced for this episode — MJSullivan hand-scraped the real content directly from the show's own site. The real canonical URL was constructed following this graph's established site convention, not independently verified against a live page. No thinkr:hasReplay or thinkr:hasTranscript is asserted, since no such URLs were captured in the source material. This episode closes a real citation gap first flagged in Frankly_71's own scopeNote and confirmed by episode number directly by MJSullivan (2026-08-31)."@en ;
    .


