tgs:Monologue.Frankly_71_WeakestLinks a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Weakest Links: Depletion, Supply Chains, and Trust"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded within days of a real, live geopolitical event — the September 2024 Israeli pager/walkie-talkie attack on Hezbollah operatives in Lebanon — Nate treats it as a real, potential 'black swan' for one of his own Four Horsemen, then broadens out into seven things he sees declining largely out of public view: oil well productivity (citing a real, contemporary ExxonMobil report on a ~15% average annual decline rate), planetary-boundary stability, the value of money (real, specific federal debt-growth figures), institutional/interpersonal/media trust, civility, collective nervous-system regulation, and — the episode's real central focus — the stability of global just-in-time supply chains and globalization itself. Applies evolutionary game theory's cooperation/selfishness/altruism/spite framework directly to the pager attack, with 'spite' — 'doing something at a cost to yourself, but a greater cost to your competitor' — offered as Nate's own real, direct read of why Israel would choose this specific, costly, escalatory tactic."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-09-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-09-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/71-weakest-links> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=izxQcGnT6P4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/Frankly71TranscriptWeakestLinks.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_100_NateHagens, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_11_InSpiteOf ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opens with real, disarming personal candor — recorded 'under the influence of Zizol,' an antihistamine for ragweed allergies, and admitting he's behind on Franklies with 'a list of 37 more' waiting. The real analytical spine is the seven-item decline list, delivered in Nate's own words as deliberately 'in no particular order' despite counting down from seven to one — oil well decline rates, planetary boundaries (explicitly naming six of the real, specific thresholds — biosphere integrity, novel entities like endocrine disruptors and plastics, land-system change, freshwater change, biogeochemical flows), the real value of money eroding under debt growth, declining trust, declining civility, nervous-system dysregulation, and finally supply-chain fragility as the real, live news-driven focus. The most direct, real personal reasoning comes in Nate's own account of why he thinks Israel chose this specific tactic — walking through the full game-theory payoff matrix (cooperation, selfishness, altruism, spite), the same real framework he had already laid out at length in Frankly_11, landing on spite as the only framework that fits: a tactic that costs the actor real reputational and commercial damage (global South buyers now wary of Israeli, UK, and US supply-chain partners) specifically because it costs the target more. Nate is careful and explicit about the limits of his own analysis here — 'I don't even want to touch' the underlying Israel-Hezbollah conflict itself, keeping the discussion scoped to what it reveals about globalization's fragility rather than the conflict's own merits. Closes by directly connecting the whole discussion back to The Great Simplification and nearshoring, naming Russia as one of the only real countries with genuine end-to-end supply-chain oversight, and previewing a real forthcoming guest, Roman Krznaric."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "dct:references to TGS_100 confirmed directly, not inferred: this episode's own real show notes link 'Four Horsemen' to a specific timestamp (18:23) within TGS_100's own real YouTube video (youtube.com/watch?v=GocuMZX3hIs&t=1103s) — the exact same video ID already used as TGS_100's own thinkr:hasReplay. 'Frankly on spite' is real Frankly #11, now built (2026-08-31) — its own real spite framework is the same one applied here to the pager attack. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Roman Krznaric — already a real, independently-flagged deferred citation elsewhere in this graph (TGS_213's own scopeNote, via his real episode #142), now built as well, since this session — reconfirmed here, not a new find. Pew Research's own three real, distinct citations on institutions/interpersonal/media trust (all in this episode's own real show notes) are a strong, direct match for the WWNS candidate Pew declining-trust piece — noted for future WWNS pipeline use, not asserted as a formal reference here since no WWNS article has used this pairing yet."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_72_WhatIfClimateChangeWasAHoax a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "What if Climate Change Was a Hoax?"@en ;
    skos:definition "Prompted directly by a real, contemporary paper on 485 million years of Earth's temperature history being misread online as proof current warming is trivial, Nate directly grants climate skeptics their entire premise for the sake of argument — assuming climate change really were a hoax — then walks through the broader ecological crisis (biodiversity collapse, ocean deoxygenation, planetary boundary breaches, microplastics) to show none of it depends on climate change being real at all."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-09-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-09-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/72-what-if-climate-change-was-a-hoax> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GNwoLw0Gu64> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/Frankly72TranscriptWhatifClimateChangeWasaHoax_.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    dct:references tgs:Human.StefanRahmstorf, tgs:Human.AlfredRusselWallace ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly corrects the misreading of the real 485-million-year paleoclimate paper at its source: the study itself actually shows Earth has never changed temperature this fast at any point in that entire record, and that the sun itself has grown roughly 50% brighter since Earth's formation, meaning lower CO2 is now required just to hold temperature steady relative to any past era — the exact opposite of what the misreading claims. He's real and direct that biodiversity today, even in its currently diminished state, remains the highest ever recorded across the fossil record, directly undercutting any simple 'more CO2 always meant more life' argument, since several of Earth's worst prior mass extinctions were themselves directly caused by rapid CO2 and volcanic outgassing events. Real, direct, striking material anchoring the human-impact half of the argument: human-made mass now outweighs all living biomass on Earth combined, plastics alone outweigh all animal life, and a real, direct clinical study found roughly 0.5% of human brain tissue by mass is now microplastic. He's real and direct that global sperm count is declining at roughly 2.6% annually, worldwide, not just regionally, with rising heat itself directly worsening fertility given how human anatomy evolved to keep testes cooler than core body temperature. A genuinely striking rhetorical move closes the biodiversity section: Nate directly reads two real, historical passages from Alfred Russel Wallace (already built) — one from 1876 describing an already 'zoologically impoverished world' after the loss of large mammals, another from 1911 directly attributing that loss to human activity — offered as real, direct proof this exact concern is roughly 150 years old, not a recent invention. Closes on real, direct rhetorical discipline: repeatedly setting aside each climate-linked ocean impact (sea level rise, AMOC slowdown, acidification) 'since that's linked to warming,' Nate is left only with overfishing and planetary boundary breaches unrelated to climate at all — six of nine already exceeded — as sufficient grounds alone for serious concern, closing with the deliberately 'valueless, prescriptionless' observation that the ecological case holds regardless of anyone's position on climate change itself."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct origin confirmed: this episode was prompted directly by Nate's own real, recent conversation with Stefan Rahmstorf (already built this session, given his own real, recurring citation pattern across multiple episodes) — his own TGS episode is not yet built despite the Human entry now existing. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Emily Judd (real, directly named paleobiologist, lead author of the real 485-million-year paper this episode responds to, not built)."@en ;
    .


