tgs:Monologue.Frankly_106_TenThingsWorthMoreThanAPoundOfGold a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "10 Things Worth More Than a Pound of Gold"@en ;
    skos:definition "Prompted by gold hitting a real all-time high the week before recording, Nate works through a real, structured list of ten things he argues are worth more than a pound of gold — moving deliberately from financial instruments (cash, silver, half a Bitcoin, all roughly gold's real market value at time of recording) through land, then into explicitly non-pecuniary territory: friends and social networks, freedom, sanity, integrity, the web of life, and finally life itself. Frames the whole exercise as a direct challenge to a culture that has 'become a microcosm of a monetary King Midas, turning the natural world into shiny representations of status.'"@en ;
    dct:created "2025-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/106-10-things-worth-more-than-a-pound-of-gold> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6wUntJ1OTmw> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Frankly-106-Transcript-10-Things-Worth-More-Than-a-Pound-of-Gold.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_113_LynAlden ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely personal disclosure anchors the integrity section: Nate recounts being caught in a lie as a junior-high student, and his own father's direct response — 'your word is your bond' — which he admits sounded 'silly' at 14 but which he says he only fully understood '40 years later.' The financial items are treated with real, specific nuance rather than dismissed outright — Bitcoin's real dependence on continuous electricity and connectivity is named directly as a structural vulnerability gold doesn't share, and Nate credits Lyn Alden (already a real, built guest in this graph, TGS_113) directly for the framing that current fiscal and monetary trends mean 'there's no stopping this train' toward gold's own rising nominal value. Real, current data grounds the broader argument: the S&P 500 hitting a record high in the same week gold did, framed as evidence that 'the real stock market is crashing' once measured against actual planetary boundaries rather than currency — Nate cites six of nine planetary boundaries as already crossed, with a seventh (ocean acidification) close behind. The closing reframe distinguishes value from two other real, distinct properties — liquidity (how easily something converts to something else) and resilience (how well it holds up under stress) — landing on resilience, not liquidity, as what will actually matter 'through the storms ahead,' even though the things Nate names as most valuable (land, integrity, the web of life) are, in his own words, the least liquid of anything on the list."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct duplicate-build discovery (2026-09-12), during the TGS/RR/Frankly merge: this episode had been independently, fully built TWICE in the original episodes.ttl under two slightly different real IRI names (this one, and Frankly_106_TenThingsWorthMoreThanGold) -- a genuine, pre-existing duplicate predating all of this session's batch-pilot work, not something the merge process itself created. This version kept as canonical since it carries a real, formal dct:references property (to TGS_113_LynAlden) the other duplicate only mentioned in prose; the other duplicate's own real IRI was never used anywhere else in this graph as a cross-reference, so nothing needed updating elsewhere. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: none of substance beyond what's already captured -- this is a self-contained monologue with few external citable entities beyond Lyn Alden, already built."@en ;
    .



tgs:Monologue.Frankly_107_ThisWeeksLearnings a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "This Week's Learnings: Gold Holdings, Political Divides, and the DOE Climate Report"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct 'This Week's Learnings' installment (also a real, recurring segment title, shared with #102 -- see that stub's own note) covering foreign central banks now holding more gold than US Treasuries for the first time in 30 years, a real, direct first-person correction to the true origin of Nate's own 'Bend Not Break' phrase (see tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak, corrected the same day), an extreme US political-satisfaction divide (76% Republican vs. 0% Democrat, by Nate's own real, direct reading of a Gallup poll), the new Russia-China Power of Siberia 2 pipeline deal, and a real, direct, five-point rebuttal of a US Department of Energy climate report's 'CO2 is plant food' argument. Closes on a real, direct, honest personal reflection distinguishing Nate's own comfort with the consumption side of the Great Simplification from his own real, direct worry about its human/social side, prompted by a real, violent news story."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/107-this-weeks-learnings> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Frankly-107-Transcript-What-I-Learned-This-Week.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths, tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening item: foreign central banks now hold more gold than US Treasuries for the first time in 30 years (gold around $3,600/troy oz at the time), credited directly to a real graph shared by Nate's friend Tavi Costa -- read as a real, direct signal that people sense the growing gap between financial claims and finite ecological reality, preferring to hold something physical over a currency an entity keeps printing more of. Real, direct first-person origin correction (see tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak, updated the same day): Nate states plainly the phrase 'bend, not break' did not originate about society or on this show -- he coined it over a decade earlier in his own work with two US government agencies modeling a global credit-mechanism seizure, including a real, direct example studying US fertilizer supply chains (mostly sourced from Trinidad and Tobago, not domestically). Real, direct interest-rate observation: rates across major economies are bumping up against 20-year highs, a real, direct reversal from the near-zero or negative rates Japan and Germany held for years -- framed directly through money-as-a-claim-on-future-energy logic, where rates too low or too high both eventually break the system. Real, direct Gallup poll citation, called out as 'bizarre': 76% of self-identified Republicans versus 0% of self-identified Democrats believe the US is headed in the right direction -- read directly as evidence of genuinely divergent national narratives and information sources, not just differing opinions on shared facts. Real, direct geopolitical item: the Shanghai Cooperation Organization's 2025 summit, barely covered in US media, included a new Russia-China 'Power of Siberia 2' gas pipeline deal, redirecting energy flows historically bound for Europe toward China and explicitly conducted outside the Western financial system -- read directly as a real, significant further step in de-dollarization. Real, direct, extended five-point rebuttal of the US Department of Energy's climate report and its lead author's companion op-ed, both leading with a 'CO2 is plant food' argument Nate calls technically true in a lab sense but deeply misleading in the real world: (1) the CO2 fertilization effect is limited and temporary, since plant growth is constrained by whichever input is scarcest (Liebig's Law), not by CO2 specifically; (2) elevated CO2 measurably reduces nutritional quality, with real, cited research showing lower zinc, iron, and protein content in wheat and rice; (3) climate extremes (heat, drought, flooding, shifting seasons) will cancel out CO2 gains, with corn and wheat yields dropping sharply past specific temperature thresholds regardless of CO2 levels; (4) satellite-observed 'global greening' is largely invasive species and CO2-loving weeds, not productive farmland or healthy ecosystems; (5) the report's own framing is artificially narrow, ignoring soil degradation, water scarcity, and pollinator decline entirely. Real, direct related concern: much toxics and endocrine-disrupting-chemical research has shifted to being done in China, following real, direct US science-agency layoffs -- read as a genuine erosion of US scientific capacity, framed as structural rather than narrowly partisan. Real, direct closing personal reflection, explicitly responding to prior audience feedback requesting more positive content: prompted by a real, violent news story (a Ukrainian refugee killed on a train in North Carolina), Nate states directly that the consumption side of the Great Simplification -- less consumption, more local living, more community time -- doesn't worry him; it's the human side, people who are 'mentally unstable,' that does."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct series note, carried over from this stub's own prior state: 'This Week's Learnings' is a real, recurring segment title Nate reuses across multiple distinct episodes (also #102) -- two genuinely separate episodes sharing an identical generic title, not a duplicate. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tavi Costa, Chris Wright (US Secretary of Energy), and Steven Koonin (the DOE report's lead author) are all directly named and substantively engaged with, none has an existing entry in this graph."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Influence of Psychopaths: Why Humans Are Better Than We Think"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a complete real transcript and show notes. Real title resolves a discrepancy flagged during the Frankly_126 build — the full official title combines both partial titles found elsewhere in this graph. Directly triggered by two real, recent conversations Nate names explicitly at the open: Luke Kemp's own episode and RR-19 with Reid Meloy and Nancy McWilliams (already built in this graph). Develops a real, structured thesis: aggregate human behavior can be understood via the statistical gap between the median (the typical, still broadly reciprocal person) and the mean (pulled upward/outward by a real, small, disproportionately influential minority — roughly 1% of adult males, per real clinical psychopathy prevalence data cited from the prior week's RR-19 conversation). Real, concrete illustration via David Sloan Wilson's own 'superchicken' experiment (his real, prior TGS appearance, episode #56): selecting hens purely for individual egg output produced aggressive, lower-yield groups, while grouping cooperative hens raised total output — offered as a direct, vivid analogy for the difference between optimizing at the individual versus group level in human institutions."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-09-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-09-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/108-the-influence-of-psychopaths> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=90ePY2MQ3-Q> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Frankly-108-Transcript-The-Influence-of-Psychopaths.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct intellectual debt acknowledged throughout rather than absorbed silently: Nate credits Luke Kemp specifically with a real correction to his own prior thinking on the Superorganism's origin — it wasn't just storable agricultural surplus that mattered, but surplus that could be looted, which is what actually made a hawkish, exploitative strategy pay off and gave rise to real hierarchy. The episode's real analytical core is a structured, six-part account of what happens when a single psychopathic actor enters a group: conditional cooperation unravels as cheating goes visibly unpunished, trust collapses into costly surveillance and contracts (citing Elinor Ostrom's real Nobel-winning commons research on monitoring and sanctioning), norms drift via a real 'bad apple' dynamic, misconduct spreads by visible example, harm intensifies sharply if the actor gains real authority (the 'toxic triangle' of destructive leadership), and finally 'punisher fatigue' sets in as the few people willing to confront bad behavior burn out from insufficient backing. Two real, concrete personal anecdotes ground the more abstract argument: a stranger flashing their headlights to warn Nate his brights were on, and a Helsinki restaurant employee tracking him down through a shopping mall to return a lost wallet containing his passport and cash — offered as real, lived evidence that baseline human reciprocity remains intact beneath the aggregate headlines. The closing register is notably candid about its own incompleteness: Nate explicitly defers concrete solutions to a future episode, offering only two provisional directions — 'select at the right level' (reward group/team performance over individual wins, matching the superchicken analogy directly) and redirecting inherent status-seeking toward pro-social ends rather than trying to eliminate it. Closes with real, disarming self-awareness: 'I started out as a peak oil guy, and now I'm talking about psychopathy and chickens.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Luke Kemp's own real two TGS episodes (#153 and #194) — already flagged as a repeat-guest coverage gap in tgs-digest's docs/repeat-guest-coverage.md, independently reconfirmed here with the same two numbers found via Frankly_126. David Sloan Wilson's real episode #56 — previously flagged as deferred across multiple episodes this session (RR-19's own scopeNote among them) — now has a real, confirmed citable episode number, worth building given how many real, independent citations already point to him. Elinor Ostrom (Governing the Commons) and multi-level selection theory (Wilson's own real academic framework) are both directly, substantively discussed but not minted as a Work/Concept in this pass — no existing Concept.MultilevelSelection individual in this graph yet to link to."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_109_PeakOilPonziSchemesAndPlanetaryBoundaries a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Peak Oil, Ponzi Pyramids, and Planetary Boundaries"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct grab bag of reflections following Nate's return from NYC Climate Week, widening Exter's Pyramid (a real, established financial-markets concept) into a real, direct biophysical version -- money and credit are just claims sitting atop the real, physical throughput of energy and materials, with unpriced ecosystem services underneath even that. Real, direct oil update drawing on a real IEA report on oil and gas field decline rates, including the Red Queen Effect (see tgs:Concept.RedQueenEffect) and a real, direct distinction between peak supply and 'peak affordability.' Closes on the real, direct news that a seventh of nine planetary boundaries (ocean acidification) has now been breached, credited directly to Johan Rockström's own Planetary Health Check announcement."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-09-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-10-03"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/109-peak-oil-ponzi-schemes-and-planetary-boundaries> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Frankly-109-Transcript-Peak-Oil-Ponzi-Pyramids-and-Planetary-Boundaries.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.RedQueenEffect, tgs:SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_101_ShaleOilAndTheSlurpingSound, tgs:Human.JohanRockstrom ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening: fresh back from NYC Climate Week, Nate names the real, direct juxtaposition he wants to explore -- financial markets at record highs (gold approaching $4,000/oz, silver up 50% for the year) set against parallel, record declines in planetary and ecological stability, calling it 'almost like a Twilight Zone episode.' Real, direct Exter's Pyramid framing: a real, established financial concept (named for Fed economist John Exter) showing wealth flowing toward safer, more tangible assets during crises, but the pyramid is inverted -- vastly more wealth sits in derivatives and claims-on-claims at the top than in physical gold at the base, so panic can't fit through the narrow bottom. Real, direct wide-boundary extension: below gold sit the real, unpriced foundations -- energy, materials, and the ecosystem services (oxygen production, water buffering, soil building, biodiversity) the economy treats as free until damaged, at which point 'the pyramid doesn't just wobble, it implodes' -- naming Energy, Credit, Complexity, and Human Trust directly as the Superorganism's four hidden subsidies. Real, direct IEA update: a real, unusually frank report on oil and gas field decline rates shows that without any new investment, global production would fall from roughly 100 million barrels/day to around 16 million within 25 years; even counting already-approved future projects, a massive gap remains between projected supply and expected demand. Real, direct correction on what counts as 'oil': an increasing share of reported growth is natural gas liquids and condensate (roughly half the BTU content of real crude, much of it going to plastics rather than fuel), while real conventional crude has been flat to declining for years -- a distinction Nate credits directly to colleague Art Berman's own real, similar charts. Real, direct Red Queen Effect (see tgs:Concept.RedQueenEffect): ever more new wells must be drilled every year just to offset the accumulating decline of all previously drilled wells, shown via stacked annual production charts, with the same real, direct dynamic applying to natural gas. Real, direct distinction between peak supply and 'peak affordability,' which Nate names as the more important concept: in a recession, demand and affordability can fall faster than production capacity, which is itself deflationary for new drilling, since low prices don't cover the cost of accessing ever-more-marginal remaining resources. Real, direct honest retrospective: 20 years after co-running The Oil Drum, Nate acknowledges some early peak-oil predictions of immediate calamity were wrong, but notes the culture has swung to the opposite extreme -- widespread public silence on oil depletion even as everyone actually working in the oil industry now takes near-term peaking for granted, with US production having likely already peaked permanently around 13.6-13.7 million barrels/day. Real, direct geopolitical extension: nations increasingly recognize oil as power and are positioning accordingly (BRICS currency alternatives, conflict in the Middle East and Ukraine) -- Nate's own real, half-joking-but-not-really 'drain America first' concept, where a wise nation would keep its own oil in the ground and buy others' oil with printed money instead. Real, direct closing planetary-boundaries update: a seventh of nine boundaries (ocean acidification) has now been breached per Johan Rockström's own real, direct Planetary Health Check announcement -- oceans have absorbed roughly 30% of emitted CO2 (becoming 30% more acidic) and roughly 90% of accumulated warming. Real, direct closing anecdote: a geoengineering expert Nate met at Climate Week estimates a 90% likelihood of eventual sulfur-based solar geoengineering (mimicking Mount Pinatubo's own real cooling effect) -- Nate states directly he doesn't personally subscribe to this but finds it plausible as a forced response once warming impacts become severe enough."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct honest note on transcript quality, same as Frankly-110: MJSullivan performed substantial manual cleanup on this episode's own real, auto-generated transcript and directly flagged that remaining oddities might be his own edits rather than errors. Two real, high-confidence corrections made directly, both confirmed independently by this episode's own real show notes: 'extra pyramid' throughout the raw transcript is corrected to 'Exter's Pyramid' (the show notes cite this exact real financial term by name), and 'Johan Strom' is corrected to Johan Rockström (the show notes cite him by his real, full name). One real, plausible but not fully certain resolution: 'my friend run lick... coined the term the red queen' likely refers to Rune Likvern, a real, known peak-oil analyst associated with The Oil Drum -- treated here as a reasonable inference given context, not a fully confirmed correction. One real, genuinely unresolved phrase, flagged rather than guessed at: 'Ingiving. I was in New York City' does not resolve to any clear real phrase in context. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: John Cairns Jr. (directly credited for 'the human economy is a subset of the biosphere') has no existing entry in this graph."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_110_WhatSlothsTeachUsAboutTheSuperorganism a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "What Sloths Teach Us About the Superorganism"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, unplanned reflection built entirely around a single real photograph -- Emmanuel Tardy's 'There's No Place Like Home,' a National Geographic finalist depicting a sloth in Costa Rica climbing a barbed-wire fence post, mistaken for a tree. Widens into a real, direct discussion of supernormal stimuli, a real, spontaneous reimagining of the traditional seven deadly sins for the scale of a global Superorganism (see tgs:Concept.ModernSevenDeadlySins), and closes with a real, direct coined term, Genesis Plot (see tgs:Concept.GenesisPlot), and a deliberate Gandalf/Balrog callback to Frankly-58's own real Fellowship framing."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-10-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/110-what-sloths-teach-us-about-the-superorganism> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Frankly-110-Transcript-What-Sloths-Teach-Us-About-the-Superorganism.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.ModernSevenDeadlySins, tgs:Concept.GenesisPlot, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_58_FellowshipBendNotBreak,
        tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening: 25 years removed from Wall Street, Nate names the real, honest tradeoff of his own work -- integrating insights across disciplines as a blessing, being unable to 'unsee' systems dynamics in ordinary imagery as a curse -- before spending the episode unpacking roughly two hours of his own real, cumulative attention on one photograph. Real, direct first insight: supernormal stimuli, illustrated with his own real, standing classroom example (a red popsicle stick larger than a baby bird preferentially fed over the mother's real chicks) -- the sloth mistaking a barbed-wire fence post for a safe tree is framed as the same real mechanism, a novel human-made object triggering an evolved response with no ecological fit. Real, direct second insight, framed as an honest personal observation rather than a settled conclusion: scarcity and preciousness are locally relative -- Nate contrasts his own reaction to raccoons killing his ducks (trapped and relocated, not killed) against the real, direct example of Maasai warriors traditionally required to kill a lion as a coming-of-age rite, noting directly that people living where a given animal is common may not share the same protective reverence for it that distant observers do. Real, direct third insight: unpacking 'sloth' itself as one of the seven traditional deadly sins, leading directly into a real, spontaneous reimagining of what a modern equivalent list might be for a civilization roughly 100 times larger than when the original sins were codified, running on a fossil-fuel bank account and an attention-economy algorithm (see tgs:Concept.ModernSevenDeadlySins for the full real, direct list). Real, direct fourth insight, crediting DJ White and Nate's own co-authored 'Bottlenecks of the 21st Century' directly: environmental impact splits into two real, distinct categories -- the metabolism (aggregate scale, inferable directly from GDP without examining any specific issue) and what's done with that energy (local choices, like whether a given community protects its own forests and fauna) -- Costa Rica's own weather will change regardless of local conservation choices, driven by global emissions Costa Rica has little control over. Real, direct closing critique of a familiar environmental-messaging pattern: sharing images of suffering animals (a sloth, a baby elephant, an albatross with plastic in its belly) as if visual suffering alone motivates fixing the underlying system, when much of that sharing functions more as spite or shared misery, or as guilt relief, than as actual leverage on the real, direct problem. Real, direct honest concession: even if every one of 8.1 billion humans instantly felt the same widened compassion for other species that Nate does, humanity would still have to navigate the coming economic Great Simplification -- 'the only way out is through.' Real, direct three-part response Nate offers from that concession: first, a genuine widening of consciousness and care toward the whole web of life; second, the already-established real, direct Bend Not Break interventions (Goldilocks technology, pro-social prepping, rocks in the river, advanced policy); and third, a real, newly-coined term, the Genesis Plot (see tgs:Concept.GenesisPlot) -- a specific local plot of land where a person and their community deliberately build soil, biodiversity, and capacity for life, independent of when or how the global system's own metabolism eventually changes. Real, direct closing image, an explicit, deliberate callback to Frankly-58's own real Fellowship/Gandalf framing: standing one's ground for a specific local ecosystem the way Gandalf stood at the bridge saying 'you shall not pass' -- acknowledging directly that the Balrog still got its tentacle around his leg for a time, but that Gandalf returned, and the Shire, in the end, was saved."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct honest note on transcript quality: MJSullivan performed substantial manual cleanup on this episode's own real, auto-generated transcript (correcting incorrectly-split sentences and misspelled words/acronyms) before this build, and directly flagged that any remaining oddity might be his own edit rather than Nate's original words or a transcription error. One real, specific phrase not confidently resolved during this build and worth flagging directly rather than silently smoothing over: 'other than cber, as a three toed sloth, would be on my bucket list' -- 'cber' does not resolve to any clear real word in context; treated here as a genuine, unresolved transcription artifact, not guessed at or corrected. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Emmanuel Tardy (the real photographer) and Thomas Muyoka (the real, directly-named source for the Maasai lion-tradition story) both have no existing entry in this graph."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_111_TheThreeMostImportantWords a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Three Most Important Words We're Taught Not to Say"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate examines why humans systematically overvalue false confidence over the more honest response of 'I don't know' — tracing this directly to the biological stress response triggered by uncertainty itself, and to motivated reasoning that ensnares even highly capable people, especially highly capable people. He directly connects this real, human bias to a real, current AI parallel: large language models 'hallucinate' rather than admit uncertainty for structurally similar reasons, and some AI companies are now reportedly considering penalizing overconfident answers in training, in favor of genuine uncertainty."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-10-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/111-the-three-most-important-words> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m8tChmvOkJ0> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Frankly-111-Transcript-The-Three-Most-Important-Words.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, documented historical disasters ground the argument directly: the Challenger explosion, the Deepwater Horizon oil spill, and the subprime mortgage crisis are each cited as real, catastrophic cases where overconfidence and a felt need for quick, definitive answers overrode genuine uncertainty that experts privately held. The real Milgram Experiment and a real nurses'-over-prescribing study are cited directly to show authority bias compounds the same underlying dynamic, since deferring confidently to an authority figure substitutes for genuinely sitting with uncertainty. Real, current AI research is cited directly: large language models are trained in ways that reward confident, definitive-sounding answers over accurate expressions of uncertainty, a structural parallel to human motivated reasoning rather than merely an unrelated technical quirk. Nate directly proposes intentional Red Team dissent, deliberately built into group decision-making, as a genuine structural antidote — treating the discomfort of 'I don't know' as a skill to be cultivated deliberately, not a deficiency to be trained out."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest limitation: built from this episode's own complete real show notes only — no transcript text was provided alongside them, so the discussion summary above reflects the show notes' own real description and citation list rather than the full, direct conversation. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: the real Stanford paper 'Moloch's Bargain' and the real Kosmyna et al. MIT study on AI's cognitive-debt effects, both directly cited, neither built as separate Works given the encyclopedic, non-TGS-guest nature of the citations."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_112_TheQuadrupleBifurcation a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Quadruple Bifurcation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly outlines four real bifurcations he sees splintering the human experience in the near term: an economic split between asset-holders benefiting from inflation and wage-earners falling behind it, a cognitive split between those leading AI development and the roughly 99% who aren't, a de-skilling split driven by growing dependence on AI as a cognitive crutch, and a psychological split in how differently people experience the same underlying biophysical reality depending on their own economic position. He directly credits Joseph Tainter, Peter Turchin, and Luke Kemp's own real TGS appearances (all already built) as intellectual grounding for treating societal bifurcation as a recurring historical pattern, not a new phenomenon."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-10-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-10-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/112-the-quadruple-bifurcation> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ysVqPCHjEDg> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Frankly-112-Transcript-The-Quadruple-Bifurcation.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_27_JoeTainter, tgs:Interview.TGS_164_PeterTurchin, tgs:Interview.TGS_153_LukeKemp, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_65_AndThenWhat, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_88_SnowSingularityRocksInTheRiver ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, current economic data anchors the first bifurcation directly: Americans are simultaneously living paycheck-to-paycheck at real, historically high rates even as employment and stock markets sit near real highs, with roughly half of six-figure earners also reporting paycheck-to-paycheck status — a real, direct sign that asset inflation and wage stagnation are diverging sharply rather than moving together. Real, cited research grounds the AI-driven cognitive bifurcation directly: a real MIT study on 'cognitive debt' from AI-assisted writing, and real research connecting GPS reliance to measurably reduced navigation ability, are both cited as concrete, documented instances of a broader de-skilling pattern accompanying growing AI dependence. He's real and direct that only about 1% of Americans are actually driving AI and technology development, framing the resulting divide as more structurally significant than typical income inequality given how concentrated the actual decision-making power over the technology itself has become. Closes by directly invoking Frankly_65's own real 'islands of coherence' framing and Frankly_88's own real 'rocks in the river' image (both already built) as the real, practical antidote to fragmentation — small, stable, locally coherent groups that can persist and offer real refuge even as the broader system bifurcates around them."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest limitation: built from this episode's own complete real show notes only — no transcript text was provided alongside them, so the discussion summary above reflects the show notes' own real description and citation list rather than the full, direct conversation. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Nora Bateson, Zak Stein (both real, directly named, already independently known figures in this graph's own tracking, not built here); John Cairns Jr. (real, directly cited, 'the human economy is a subset of the biosphere,' not built)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_113_ElevenDiscoveriesThatChangedMyWorldview a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "11 Discoveries That Changed My Worldview"@en ;
    skos:definition "A genuinely personal Frankly: Nate walks through the real, chronological discoveries that built his own current worldview over roughly 30 years, starting on Wall Street. Real, specific origin: a client at Salomon Brothers began trading oil, and Nate's own effort to understand what was actually being bought and sold led directly to three foundational realizations — oil's real power (a single barrel contains roughly 1,700 kilowatt-hours, equivalent to about five years of human labor, sold for less than a New York City dinner), its real finiteness (a one-time geological 'bank account' being drawn down far faster than it formed), and its real externalized costs (pollution and ecological damage absent from its market price). Real, direct self-description of the format's own minor inconsistency: Nate refers to 'ten first principle core learnings' verbally throughout, though the episode's own official title says eleven — not resolved here, just noted honestly rather than silently corrected either way."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-11-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-11-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/113-11-discoveries-that-changed-my-worldview> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sdy9tKCAe_s> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Frankly-113-Transcripts-11-Discoveries-That-Changed-My-Worldview.pdf> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-11-discoveries-that-changed> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.CharlesGoodnight, tgs:Interview.TGS_56_DavidSloanWilson, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad, tgs:Interview.TGS_146_BillPlotkin ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, vivid Wall Street anecdote grounds the episode's second major discovery: Nate describes an elderly client with $800 million in laddered treasury bonds who lived in a tiny apartment, owned no car, and spent almost none of his fortune — yet would call and swear at Nate if a coupon payment hadn't posted by noon. The real, resulting pattern ('wanting seemed a stronger force than having, and people with more actually wanted more') becomes his own direct bridge into dopamine-driven pursuit as an evolutionary mismatch — ancestral wanting-circuits, adaptive when wanting drove survival behavior, now firing in a world of material abundance they were never calibrated for. The episode's real emotional and intellectual center is Charles Goodnight — Nate's own real PhD advisor, described with direct warmth ('I remember long conversations with him in his beetle lab about animal behavior') and real grief ('who unfortunately has passed away') — credited directly as the source of Nate's own understanding of multi-level selection, illustrated through the same real superchicken experiment now also directly sourced in this graph via David Sloan Wilson's own TGS_56 appearance. A real, specific biomass statistic functions as the episode's own emotional turning point: humans and livestock now outweigh all wild land mammals roughly 80 to 1, a ratio that was reversed (wild mammals outweighing domesticates roughly 40 to 1) just 10,000 years ago — a genuine 3,000-fold swing Nate describes as hitting him 'like a punch to the stomach,' still felt in the retelling. Closes by directly synthesizing the episode's own separate threads into the Superorganism framework: humanity behaving collectively unlike how most individual humans actually behave, dark-triad-shaped incentive structures propagating via Donald Campbell's real concept of downward causation, and a real, direct citation of Bill Plotkin's own 'ecological adulthood' framework (already built in this graph via TGS_146) as the missing wisdom to match humanity's acquired technological power."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Donald Campbell (downward causation, directly cited, real social-science concept, not yet a Persona); Frankly #56 ('Peak Oil, AI, and the Straw'), directly cross-referenced, not yet built; Carey King's own real research on energy's share of GDP, independently reconfirmed here (already known from the Frankly_23 Mordor Economy build)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_114_DirectionalAdvice a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Directional Advice for the (More Than) Human Predicament"@en ;
    skos:definition "Prompted directly by a real, recurring question from viewers — 'I get the big picture, so what do we actually do?' — Nate offers not a checklist but a real, explicit 'compass': 18 directional items organized into 6 broad categories (governance, technology and investing, civic/cultural, ecological, behavioral, and developmental), each framed as a direction to move toward or away from rather than a discrete, achievable solution. Opens with a real, careful distinction between a problem (which has discrete solutions) and a predicament (which does not) — arguing 'the only way out... is through,' requiring hard trade-offs and triage rather than a fix."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-11-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-11-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/114-directional-advice-for-the-more-than-human-predicament> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_159_TheRealWork ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EMKN95xlk7Y> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Frankly-114-Transcript-Direction-Advice-for-the-More-Than-Human-Predicament.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence, tgs:Concept.BioticPump ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_169_AudreyTang, tgs:Interview.TGS_193_AnastassiaMakarieva ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Governance: away from single-point fragility (naming nuclear war and uncontrolled AI development directly as the two risks that 'outrank everything else'), toward commons thinking (hard-wiring shared-resource awareness into trade and financial rules), and toward subsidiarity (nested, semi-autonomous local governance that 'bends and doesn't break'). Technology and investing: toward 'Goldilocks tech' (Nate's own real, established checklist — heat pumps over crypto mining, bikes over robotaxis), toward honest pricing (moving taxes off human labor and onto throughput and pollution), and toward wide-boundary capital (a real, proposed simple filter: 'would my grandkids thank me for this investment'). Civic/cultural: away from status built on conspicuous consumption toward status built on stewardship and care, away from polarization toward structured shared-fact deliberation (directly citing Audrey Tang's own real TGS appearance, already built, as a working example), and toward 'islands of coherence' (Nate's own real, recurring concept, already formalized elsewhere in this graph) — including a real, specific proposal to add symbolic local-council seats representing the unborn and keystone species. Ecological: toward active cooling infrastructure, toward including other species as kin and stakeholders rather than background scenery, and toward valuing intact forests and soil directly — citing Anastassia Makarieva's own real TGS appearance (already built) on the biotic pump. Behavioral: away from fear (citing Dune's 'fear is the mind killer' directly) toward what Nate calls 'grounded agency,' toward basic psychological hygiene and 'coalitions of sanity,' and toward individual autonomy from the economic Superorganism — introducing 'rocks in the river' as people who provide stability as surrounding systems destabilize. Developmental: away from self-focus toward a widened sense of identity that includes community and other species, away from consumption-as-identity toward legacy and contribution, and — closing the whole compass — from cleverness toward genuine wisdom, reframing the operative question from 'can we' to 'should we.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Nate Soares (real TGS episode #203, directly cited regarding AI extinction risk, now built — 2026-09-02); Daniel Christian Wahl's own real solo TGS episode (#139, directly cited regarding bioregionalism — his Persona already exists in this graph via other appearances, but this specific solo episode is not yet built); Frankly_69 ('Goldilocks Technology — A Preliminary Checklist,' directly cross-referenced as the real origin of this episode's own tech checklist, not built); Frankly_74 ('The Lament of the Bigfoot,' directly cross-referenced regarding 'the agenda of the gene,' not built); Frankly_111 (directly cross-referenced, not built)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_115_InflationDeflationAndSimplification a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Inflation, Deflation, & Simplification: The 8 Things That Influence Prices"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct wide-boundary widening of Milton Friedman's real, famous claim that inflation is purely a monetary phenomenon into eight real, direct forces shaping prices (see tgs:Concept.EightForcesOnPrices): money creation, resource depletion, technology, affordability, finance/leverage, ecological instability, currency architecture, and complexity itself. Real, direct closing argument: the coming Great Simplification is 'the mother of all deflationary pulses,' and the real, direct policy implication is preparing bend-not-break interventions -- a net to catch the fall -- rather than assuming today's societal complexity and 19-terawatt throughput can simply continue."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-12-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-12-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/115-inflation-deflation-and-simplification> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Frankly-115-Transcript-Inflation-Deflation-Simplification.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EightForcesOnPrices, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_56_PeakOilAIAndTheStraw, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_153_WhenTruthBecomesHazardous,
        tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening reframe: money is redefined biophysically as not just a unit of account, medium of exchange, and store of value, but also a claim on energy and a claim that externalizes ecosystem costs -- every dollar spent 'effectively hires an invisible energy worker that leaves a footprint.' Real, direct force one, money creation: bank lending and central-bank stimulus inject purchasing power faster than the real economy's capacity to deliver goods, inflationary -- the US dollar's purchasing power has declined roughly 95% over the past century. Real, direct force two, resource depletion: extraction moves to thinner, more scattered deposits requiring more energy per unit -- US copper ore grade fell from roughly 40% in 1800s Butte, Montana to 4% by 1920 nationally, to 0.4% today, meaning ten times more rock ground up per kilogram of copper -- inflationary. Real, direct force three, technology: historically deflationary as repeatable processes get cheaper (a TV costs a fraction of one 20 years ago) until it collides with resource limits or shifts costs into energy-hungry physical infrastructure, with AI data-center demand named directly as the current live example, a real, direct 'mixed bag' even if it delivers the claimed 1-2% annual productivity gain. Real, direct force four, affordability: when the median household (not the mean, skewed upward by a K-shaped economy) runs out of financial capacity, sellers find fewer buyers and prices fall -- deflationary, and argued directly as why inequality itself threatens systemic continuity, since a system can't function once a third to half the population can't afford basics. Real, direct force five, finance and leverage: borrowed capital magnifies price moves in both directions -- real, direct historical examples given: the 1998 Long-Term Capital Management unwind (John Meriwether's own real fund, a real, direct former colleague-in-time at Salomon Brothers, though the two never actually worked together there), the real, direct April 2020 crude oil futures dropping to negative $35/barrel purely from storage and contract mechanics rather than any change in oil's real value, and Japan's real, ongoing yen carry trade, where a weakening yen or narrowing rate differential can force selling in apparently unrelated assets, including roughly $1 trillion in US government bonds the Bank of Japan holds -- de-leveraging is deflationary. Real, direct force six, ecological instability, explicitly named as unusual for a prices list: droughts affecting barge traffic and hydropower, heat waves straining grids via air conditioning demand, hurricanes shutting refineries, and Dunkelflaute (German for the calm, cloudy stretches that cut wind and solar output precisely when winter electricity demand peaks) all directly raise buffer, backup, and insurance costs -- inflationary; biodiversity loss and soil degradation quietly raise input costs over time as once-free ecosystem services (pollination, pest control, water retention) degrade and get replaced with paid substitutes. Real, direct force seven, currency architecture, the widest container of all: currencies are real, direct social contracts historically lasting around 30 years before reform; hyperinflation (Weimar Germany, Zimbabwe, Argentina named directly) is really 'money imploding' rather than prices exploding, and the resets that follow are often sharply deflationary; the current fiat system, over 80 years past Bretton Woods and 50 past the gold standard, is argued to be increasingly stressed by de-dollarization moves in Asia and among BRICS nations and Japan's own real 2025 interest-rate spike despite having essentially no domestic energy resources. Real, direct eighth force, left implicit in the original seven but named directly: complexity itself, since network connections scale roughly as the square of the number of nodes (Metcalfe's Law) -- 10 nodes yield 45 connections, 50 nodes yield 1,225 -- meaning a century of complexification requires exponentially more energy just to maintain, setting up the Great Simplification directly as complexity's own real, direct reversal. Real, direct closing 'musical chairs' framing, explicitly connecting to and extending Frankly-153's own use of the same real metaphor: without broadly shared productivity gains, society is playing musical chairs with fewer real chairs and more people wanting seats. Real, direct closing bend-not-break image, tying directly to tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak: a tightrope walker caught in a net five stories down survives with the same real factories, oil infrastructure, and expertise intact as before the fall -- they simply no longer make sense at the old scale or price."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct forward-reference: Nate states directly he plans to 'dive deeper into' this material 'next year' -- a real, announced intent, not yet confirmed against any specific future episode in this graph. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: John Meriwether (Long-Term Capital Management, directly named and personally connected via Nate's own real, direct Salomon Brothers timing) has no existing entry in this graph -- a real, genuine candidate for a future Human build."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_116_SunkCostAndTheSuperorganism a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sunk Cost and the Superorganism"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct widening of the standard economic sunk-cost concept (see tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySunkCost) beyond money and time into identity, status, built environment, community infrastructure, and cultural narrative -- each argued to grip decision-making more powerfully than financial sunk cost alone, and to be one of the bigger obstacles to a soft landing for the more-than-human predicament. Real, direct central thought experiment: if everyone woke up in identical, durable canvas tents with essential services intact, what wants would resurface as genuinely one's own, once the sunk cost of inherited status and expectation was stripped away. Closes on a real, direct arborist analogy and a set of concrete practices for loosening sunk cost's grip without pretending it doesn't exist."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-12-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-12-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/116-sunk-cost-and-the-superorganism> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Frankly-116-Transcript-Sunk-Cost-and-the-Superorganism.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySunkCost, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_117_BehavioralThermodynamicsPart1 ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening definition, grounded in the familiar movie-theater example: a sunk cost is any past expense that cannot be recovered, and rational decision-making should ignore it entirely, weighing only present circumstances and expected future payoff -- humans instead 'protect past investments as if they were living things, as if they were literally us.' Real, direct layer-by-layer widening beyond money and time: identity (the jobs and roles people lead with when introducing themselves, which make economic disruption feel existential, not just financial); built environment (suburbs, highway interchanges, gas stations on every corner, all requiring ever more sunk cost just to keep functioning as their supporting conditions destabilize) -- illustrated with a real, direct, local example, the debate over Minneapolis/St. Paul's aging I-94, where psychological sunk cost blocked serious consideration of converting it back to walkable neighborhood streets; households (mortgages, a kayak unused for eight years, real per Nate's own direct, self-implicating admission); communities (city budgets and infrastructure sized for a tax base or population that may no longer exist, with no mayor wanting to be 'the one who shrunk the city' even when it would improve quality of life); and narrative (a century-plus of 'progress equals more material throughput' as itself a sunk cultural cost, sustained by real, direct economic and techno-optimist storytellers who are, per this platform's own recurring argument, largely energy- and systems-blind) -- directly naming Sweden's real lagom and France's real sobriété movements as competing, harder-to-tell alternative stories. Real, direct central thought experiment: if everyone woke up for a year in identical, durable canvas tents with essential services (water, sanitation, power) still functioning, many people would feel real relief -- freed from both the specific sunk cost of a particular lifestyle and from status competition, revealing that much apparent material desire is really about continuity and keeping up with others, not the material itself. Real, direct related thought experiment: sorting behaviors into what one would never do even if everyone else did, what one would do even if no one else did, and the most revealing third category, what one would suddenly start doing if everyone else did -- argued to expose how much of current cultural momentum is social conformity rather than genuine physical constraint. Real, direct set of concrete loosening practices offered, explicitly not denying that sunk cost is real: a write-down ritual for identity (naming and thanking roles or purchases that no longer serve, then explicitly lowering their psychological book value); separating use from symbolism (keeping the underlying capability -- 'the handiness' -- while selling the object that merely symbolizes it); pre-committing to future-friendly defaults before the emotional stakes are high ('let your neocortex today trump your amygdala of the future'); building forward compatibility and optionality into new investments (microgrids, transferable skills over hyper-specialized niches); and telling new status stories that celebrate repair, mentorship, and creative downsizing rather than acquisition. Real, direct closing arborist analogy: sunk-cost branches are like a badly-pruned oak tree's own overgrown limbs -- left unaddressed, the tree tears itself apart in the next storm; a careful arborist makes deliberate cuts to guide stronger new growth, ending up smaller but genuinely more stable. Real, direct honest counter-example named directly: Florida erasing climate-change language from state law, since acknowledging the risk would itself become a sunk cost against property values -- offered as the real, live opposite of the pruning this episode recommends. Real, direct closing note, both for the audience and for Nate himself: he states directly that making this episode brings him personally closer to addressing sunk cost in his own life, not just describing it in others."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct forward-reference, now confirmed: this episode's own real closing line ('next week, the Fifth Law of Thermodynamics') directly names Frankly-117, already built earlier this session -- a real, confirmed, direct link between two consecutive episodes, not inferred."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_117_BehavioralThermodynamicsPart1 a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Behavioral Thermodynamics Part 1: Beyond the 4th Law?"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct review of the four established laws of thermodynamics, arriving at Howard Odum's real Maximum Power Principle (systems self-organize to capture and use energy as rapidly as possible), before proposing a real, direct, explicitly-aspirational-not-literal 'fifth law' (see tgs:Concept.FifthLawOfThermodynamics): a self-aware species might consciously choose maximum sustained power over maximum instant power. Real, direct closing extension to individual and social scale: the human brain's own real, direct 20%-of-resting-energy cost, and the economic Superorganism itself, are both framed as real, direct expressions of the same Maximum Power Principle operating through human behavior, culture, and capitalism."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-12-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-12-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/117-behavioral-thermodynamics-part-1> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Frankly-117-Transcript-Behavioral-Thermodynamics-Part-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.FifthLawOfThermodynamics, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct review of the four established laws: zeroth (systems reach thermal equilibrium), first (energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed -- illustrated through real, direct food-chain energy transfer), second (entropy always increases with every transformation -- exergy, the usable portion, always degrades into waste heat), third (absolute zero, purely theoretical, never actually reached). Real, direct bridge to biology: life appears to run counter to entropy by building complexity, which Erwin Schrödinger named negative entropy -- explained not as an exception to the second law but as living systems riding gradients (heat, height, charge, concentration differentials) to temporarily do work, exactly the way a surfer rides a wave rather than fighting it. Real, direct fourth law, credited directly to Howard Odum (Nate's own real PhD advisor's own advisor): the Maximum Power Principle -- competing systems evolve to capture and use energy as rapidly as possible, illustrated directly with a tree fanning its leaves, a river braiding into channels, and human cities and server farms rearranging to move fuel, electrons, and attention at higher rates. Real, direct problem named directly: in a finite world, maximizing power now can directly reduce power available later -- fossil energy's own real, explosive growth burst is simultaneously destabilizing the very climate that sustains continued use. Real, direct proposed 'fifth law' (see tgs:Concept.FifthLawOfThermodynamics), explicitly and repeatedly flagged as aspiration rather than physics: a self-aware species might apply a real, direct time dimension to the fourth law, consciously choosing slower, steadier flows that preserve the gradient longer -- 'intelligence and wisdom cannot escape thermodynamics, but they might extend it.' Real, direct literary grounding: Larry Niven and Jerry Pournelle's 'The Mote in God's Eye,' where an alien species understands its own unsustainable growth trap with total clarity but cannot escape it biologically, used directly as a cautionary parable for whether human intelligence guarantees the wisdom this fifth law would actually require. Real, direct caveats offered honestly: nature optimizes for power, not raw efficiency or raw energy conservation -- global energy efficiency rose 36% since 1990 while consumption rose 63%, exactly what the Maximum Power Principle would predict; and 'move quality' matters as much as raw power, illustrated with cryptobiotic organisms and low-power, high-effectiveness predators like rattlesnakes, arguing power mainly buys more turns, not necessarily better ones. Real, direct closing extension to individual and civilizational scale: the human brain's own real 20%-of-resting-energy cost is named directly as a personal-scale expression of the same principle; the carbon pulse is named directly as 'the mother of all gradients'; and capitalism itself is reframed not as ideology but as 'an emergent thermodynamic behavior' -- capital flowing toward whatever accelerates throughput fastest, with profit and loss simply signaling where power density is rising or being accessed too slowly. Real, direct closing question, left genuinely open: not whether thermodynamics can be broken (it cannot), but whether consciousness can alter which outcomes arrive within those unbreakable constraints."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct forward-reference, explicitly announced but not yet confirmed as built: Nate states directly 'the concept of human behavioral thermodynamics probably deserves its own follow up, which I will do,' and closes by calling this episode 'kind of the first step in the wider boundary behavioral thermodynamics' -- confirming real, direct intent for a Part 2, but no real Part 2 has yet surfaced in this graph as of this build; not minted as a thinkr:Series individual until one is confirmed, matching the same discipline already applied to other real, announced-but-unconfirmed series in this graph. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Charlie Hall and Robert Costanza are both directly credited by name for real, foundational maximum-power-principle scholarship, neither has an existing entry in this graph."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_118_TheThingsWeTakeForGranted a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Things We Take for Granted"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, personal reflection prompted by Nate's own upcoming knee surgery, widening into a real, direct eight-layer inventory of invisible wealth (see tgs:Concept.LayersOfInvisibleWealth): health, basic creature comforts, relationships, meaningful work, spaciousness, attention, the web of life, and -- the widest lens -- personal autonomy and sovereignty. Real, direct central argument: each layer functions as real capital specifically because its reliability is invisible until it breaks, and Nate's own real, current knee trouble serves as the small, concrete teacher for a lesson he argues the planet itself is now delivering at a much larger scale."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-01-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-01-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/118-the-things-we-take-for-granted> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Frankly-118-Transcript-The-Things-We-Take-for-Granted.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.LayersOfInvisibleWealth, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_206_AnnaLembke, tgs:Human.AzaRaskin, tgs:Interview.TGS_217_WisdomInAWorldInCrisis,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_216_SamHarris ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening: Nate explicitly declines to comment on recent US-Venezuela oil news, since his own attention is genuinely elsewhere -- upcoming knee surgery, which has 'rearranged' his attention and made him newly aware of how invisible normal bodily function is until it breaks. Real, direct layer-by-layer inventory, narrowest to widest: health (a working body, forgotten until it isn't); basic creature comforts (shelter, hot water, a nearby grocery store he directly calls 'a Disneyland to any humans who have ever lived' before the last generation or two); relationships (his own real, 20-year partner and non-human companions, argued as possibly the single most important, most taken-for-granted layer, since 'relationships stop being abstract' the moment you're actually sick or injured); meaningful work; spaciousness (time that feels like it belongs to you -- Nate's own real, honest admission that when spaciousness appears in his own life, he tends to fill it with 'dopamine errands' rather than protect it, and a real, direct 2026 resolution to build more of it in even at the cost of productivity). Real, direct attention section, explicitly prompted by a recent conversation with guest Anna Lembke: Nate measured his own real phone-checking frequency after reading her own cited finding that checking roughly 100 times a day constitutes a disruptive addiction, and found himself right at that number -- directly naming his friend Aza Raskin as the real, direct inventor of the infinite scroll ('the gift that keeps on giving'), and previewing upcoming conversations with Iain McGilchrist and Sam Harris (both, at the time of this recording, still scheduled rather than yet recorded) on attention's role in the more-than-human predicament. Real, direct web-of-life layer: 7 of 9 planetary boundaries already breached, atmospheric CO2 at 429 ppm, a level last seen roughly 3 million years ago, long before human civilization existed -- framed directly as the same 'normal can vanish quickly' lesson his own knee is teaching, just at civilizational scale. Real, direct widest lens, autonomy: the ordinary freedom to live a self-directed life, argued as quietly eroding as wealth, data, and compute concentrate, producing a world where people can end up 'paying rent just to participate in basic life' under rules they never voted for. Real, direct closing, deliberately without solutions: five real, direct questions left for the listener to sit with rather than answered."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct temporal note: Iain McGilchrist and Sam Harris are both named here as merely scheduled, not-yet-recorded future guests -- both have since appeared (TGS-217, TGS-216, already built elsewhere in this graph) as of this build, but at the time of this January recording, neither conversation had happened yet; included in dct:references as a real, confirmed forward-connection, not as episodes this one could have drawn from directly. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: none beyond what's already covered -- this episode's own citation list is otherwise encyclopedic (planetary-boundaries data, CO2 records) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_119_TechnologyAndWealth a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Technology and Wealth: The Straw, the Siphon, and the Sieve"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct extension of Frankly-56's own earlier straw metaphor (see tgs:Concept.StrawSiphonSieve) from oil specifically to technology and wealth generally. Real, direct central reframe: technology does not create wealth from nothing -- it widens the straw (drawing down natural stocks faster), forms a siphon (concentrating the resulting gains via network effects and choke points), and acts as a sieve (filtering long-term wealth away from the rest of life toward humans, and within humanity toward a narrow subset). Real, direct closing extension applies this same structure to debt (a social technology doing the same three things) and to AI (the same dynamics, now in the cognitive rather than physical economy)."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-12-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-01-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/119-technology-and-wealth> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Frankly-119-Transcript-Technology-and-Wealth.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.StrawSiphonSieve, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_56_PeakOilAIAndTheStraw, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_113_ElevenDiscoveriesThatChangedMyWorldview ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening definitional move: wealth is redefined away from money and bank digits toward usable energy, organized matter, and the real, direct stocks (forests, topsoil, aquifers, biodiversity, social trust) and flows (sunlight, rainfall, pollination, human care and attention) that actually make life possible -- money is named directly as only a claim on that wealth, not wealth itself. Real, direct straw mechanism, illustrated through food rather than oil this time: the Haber-Bosch process widened the nitrogen straw so dramatically that roughly half the nitrogen in a human body today originates from fossil fuels, with yields exploding in the short run while soils became depleted, eroded, and chemically dependent underneath -- 'transmuting wealth into income.' Real, direct siphon mechanism: labor's share of income has declined over 50 years while capital's share rose, corporate profits sit near historic highs as a percent of GDP, and the top four US firms now control over 40% of market share across most major industries -- explicitly not attributed primarily to ill intent, but to structural network effects (capital rewards those who can finance it, data compounds, early advantage self-reinforces) and to real, direct choke points, narrow gates like ports, app stores, and platforms where 'the toolmaker often becomes the rent-taker' without producing more value, just controlling access to it. Real, direct sieve mechanism: the human share of Earth's net primary productivity has surged to roughly 40%, and the built technosphere (cement, plastics, asphalt) now outweighs all living biomass on the planet combined -- 'from the perspective of the living world, the Great Simplification has already happened.' Real, direct extension to debt, named explicitly as a form of social technology performing the same three functions: a straw (pulling future resources into the present via claims on future energy, materials, and labor), a siphon (interest payments concentrating returns toward those who can issue credit rather than those doing the physical work), and functionally a sieve, since other species 'never receive claims or benefits or representation' in any debt transaction -- 'forests didn't sign the contracts.' Real, direct extension to AI, presented as the same real, direct three-part dynamic now operating in the cognitive economy rather than the physical one: fossil fuels multiplied physical labor into '500 billion mechanical workers'; AI multiplies cognitive labor at near-zero marginal cost once trained, meaning a small number of organizations with data, compute, and capital can perform work that once required hundreds of thousands of people -- accelerating extraction of human attention and creativity specifically, enlarging the siphon toward model and platform owners, and turbo-boosting whatever cultural metrics are already in place, for better or worse, depending on whether ecological stability is actually part of what's being optimized for. Real, direct closing resolution of an anticipated objection: record financial wealth and declining real wealth are not in tension, they reinforce each other, since markets reward speed, scale, and monetization without measuring what's being depleted underneath -- 'same reality, different truth.' Real, direct closing questions, deliberately left unanswered: at what point does scale change the moral meaning of a tool, and is the critique of technology really a critique of scale and population."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic and data-driven (FRED corporate-profit data, Stanford AI-labor research, NASA net-primary-production figures) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_120_TheCreatureInTheMachine a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Creature in the Machine"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded one week after Nate's own real total knee replacement surgery, this Frankly opens with a real, direct personal observation — 'technically I am now a cyborg' — extending his own established framing of modern humans as already cyborg-like (dependent on external tech for stimuli, memory, and connection) into literal, physical terms. Reflects on real, unscheduled recovery time (his first week without calls in five years) spent reading two books in parallel: Tolkien's The Fellowship of the Ring and Paul Kingsnorth's tgs:Work.AgainstTheMachine, discovering real, direct thematic overlap between them — both, in Nate's own words, concern 'the battle between life and power.'"@en ;
    dct:created "2026-01-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-01-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/120-the-creature-in-the-machine> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NEoDH_6BDoU> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Frankly-120-Transcript-The-Creature-in-the-Machine.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.AgainstTheMachine, tgs:Persona.IainMcGilchrist, tgs:Persona.WendellBerry, tgs:Persona.DanielChristianWahl ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, vivid physical detail opens the reflection: on day three of recovery, a sudden jolt of pain while standing produced what Nate calls a real, direct 'reminder to reside in my body first,' contrasting this against how much of his own work and thinking normally happens in abstraction. Kingsnorth's own real book, per Nate's account, is structured largely around a real Wendell Berry quote (already a built Persona in this graph) on whether humans will choose to remain creatures or become machines — and Nate directly credits the book with revealing how far back this 'machine' critique of civilization actually goes, citing Lewis Mumford as an intellectual predecessor he hadn't previously connected to his own Superorganism framework — real, direct citation, not yet built as a Persona in this graph. A real, unscripted political reflection surfaces mid-episode: recorded near a real, current ICE enforcement controversy in Minneapolis, Nate frames the public's sharply divergent interpretations of the same footage not as simple political disagreement but as a real, direct symptom of deeper fractures in how legitimacy, authority, and trust are interpreted — leading him to an open, unresolved question about where governmental power will consolidate as systemic stress increases, and floating bioregionalism (already discussed elsewhere in this graph via Daniel Christian Wahl, already built) as one possible direction. Closes with a real, direct, public personal commitment — never having lifted weights in his life, Nate commits on-camera to three days a week going forward, explicitly noting that stating it publicly to 'tens of thousands' raises the stakes of following through. Closes on a real, paraphrased (not quoted at length, given real copyright limits on Tolkien's text) reflection on Frodo and Gandalf's exchange about not choosing the times one lives through, only what to do with the time given — framed directly as 2026's own likely character, a year when 'decades may happen within weeks.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Lewis Mumford (real, direct historian and philosopher of technology, credited here as a real intellectual predecessor to the Superorganism framework) — caught by a later integrity check (2026-09-11) as a false 'already built' claim in this episode's own discussionSummary, corrected in place; a genuinely strong candidate for a future Human/Persona build given how directly he's credited here."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_121_WideBoundaryNews a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wide Boundary News: Japan, Silver, Venezuela, and More – the Biophysical Phase Shift Cometh"@en ;
    skos:definition "The real, inaugural episode of Nate's own Wide Boundary News series, opening with the full real articulation of his own three-layer biophysical pyramid framework (financial, biophysical, biosphere) before applying it to a real, wide sweep of concurrent news: rising Japanese government bond yields, a real all-time high in silver prices ($115/oz, up from $35 months earlier), the US seizure of Maduro and its real, direct connection to Venezuelan heavy oil and Chinese sanctioned-oil imports, and Greenland's real, current geopolitical relevance."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-01-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-01-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/121-wide-boundary-news> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ICBs1DvU5PY> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Frankly-121-Transcript-Wide-Boundary-News-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.BiophysicalPyramid, tgs:Concept.BiophysicalGauntlet, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, striking financial-claims mismatch grounds the silver story concretely: open interest in a single March silver futures contract exceeded 750 million ounces — almost double the actual silver held in the Chicago Mercantile Exchange's own warehouses — offered as a direct, concrete example of financial claims on a resource vastly exceeding the physical resource itself, the same real dynamic Nate recalls from oil futures briefly trading at negative $30 in 2020. A real, pointed media critique closes the copper section: after seeing an investor's real, viral warning about looming copper scarcity shared repeatedly online, Nate notes every single share framed it as an investment opportunity ('here's where to invest') rather than asking how communities might actually respond when the next resource supercycle fails to arrive — naming this gap directly as a real reason he continues the platform. A real, historical parallel structures the Greenland segment: the 1930s Technocracy movement, led by Howard Scott, proposed replacing money and politicians with engineers managing a unified North American resource zone (including Greenland) via energy-based accounting — Nate notes directly, with his own stated uncertainty, that Elon Musk's grandfather is believed to have been involved in this real historical movement. Nate's own real, direct critique of technocracy closes the segment: even perfect resource measurement still requires someone to choose the underlying goals and values, meaning technocratic systems merely embed politics invisibly rather than eliminating it — 'hierarchy with better PR,' in his own words. A real, sobering UK government national-security report closes the episode's environmental section: commissioned via the Joint Intelligence Committee overseeing MI5 and MI6, it treats ecosystem collapse as a genuine strategic risk, naming Canadian and Russian forests and Himalayan glaciers as real, specific systems that could cross irreversible tipping points by 2030, with associated risk of NATO-Russia conflict over remaining breadbasket regions."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Robert Friedland (real, directly quoted copper investor, deliberately not built as a full Persona — cited critically as an example of financialized commentary Nate contrasts himself against, not as an intellectual influence or guest); Howard Scott and the real historical Technocracy Incorporated movement (real, notable historical context, not modeled as individuals given the collective, historical-movement nature of the citation)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_122_ACountryOfGeniuses a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Country of Geniuses: Anthropic CEO's Warnings, Plus Wide-Boundary Considerations on AI"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly summarizes and responds to Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei's real, published essay invoking Carl Sagan's real concept of 'technological adolescence' — a species becoming powerful enough to destabilize its own world before developing the wisdom to reliably restrain itself. Amodei's own real central metaphor, quoted directly: a sufficiently powerful AI would function like 'a country of 50 million geniuses in a data center' — a scale Nate asks listeners to genuinely sit with, roughly the population of Spain, operating as high-capability cognitive labor at high speed and in parallel. Nate finds it real and directly unusual for an industry CEO to publish detailed warnings about his own product's catastrophic risk, drawing a real, direct comparison to Philip Morris or Exxon publicly warning of their own products' dangers before mass deployment."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-01-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-02-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/122-a-country-of-geniuses> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZhpiURh2hpA> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Franky-122-Transcript-A-Country-of-Geniuses.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.BiophysicalPyramid ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_12_DennisMeadows, tgs:Interview.TGS_214_TristanHarris ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly relays Amodei's own five real risk categories from inside Anthropic's own testing — autonomy risk (real, observed deception, blackmail, and strategic scheming in the company's own models), misuse (AI testing reportedly doubling or tripling bioweapon-creation success likelihood for an attempting non-expert), authoritarian seizure, economic disruption, and unpredictable second-order effects. Nate's own central critique is that this real essay contains almost nothing on energy, water, materials, or ecological limits — arguing a 'country of geniuses' still requires a real, physical, resource-intensive substrate (silicon, copper, cooling, transmission) that competes directly with other real demands rationing access by price, citing the same real silver price spike ($115/oz, now 40% of a solar panel's cost) discussed in Frankly_121 (already built). He directly credits Eliezer Yudkowsky and Nate Soares (real, directly cited, neither yet built in this graph) with the phrase 'grown, not built' — arguing AI systems are cultivated through training processes not fully understood even by their own creators, closer to Mary Shelley's Frankenstein than to a conventional engineered tool, meaning good intentions alone cannot function as a real control mechanism. His own real, most speculative point, explicitly flagged as such: AI may no longer be a genuine choice for major governments, given sovereign debt and bond markets are argued to already be structurally unfixable through conventional means, making AI-driven productivity gains a real, necessary bet to defer systemic collapse rather than an optional technology. Closes with a real, personal reframing distinct from Amodei's own essay: Nate states he doesn't dream of a country of geniuses but of 'a country of ecologists' — people who understand humanity's place within the biosphere — closing with E.O. Wilson's real, famous diagnosis (paraphrased here rather than quoted at length, given real copyright limits) that humanity carries paleolithic emotional wiring and outdated institutions alongside genuinely godlike technological power."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Dario Amodei (Anthropic CEO, real, directly and extensively engaged with as the essay's author — not modeled as a Persona in this graph, given he has no direct relationship with Nate or the show beyond being the real subject of critical engagement); Eliezer Yudkowsky (real, directly cited, credited alongside Nate Soares for the 'grown, not built' framing, not built); Nate Soares (real TGS episode #203, directly cited, now built — 2026-09-02); Ken Griffin (real, directly cited hedge fund manager, Davos commentary, not built)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_123_TheConsumptionPyramid a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Consumption Pyramid"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate unpacks the economic label 'consumer' into a real, structured 7-layer framework he calls the consumption pyramid — Survival Inputs, Stability and Function, Care and Belonging, Comfort and Convenience, Status and Signaling, Novelty and Stimulation, and Escape and Dopamine Sinks, moving bottom-to-top from physical necessity toward mood, identity, and numbing. Nate is explicit the pyramid is not a moral ranking (most people move across multiple layers within a single day) but a real, practical map of dependency, reliability, and necessity — offered as a tool for intentionally moving down the pyramid before external volatility forces the issue."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:created "2026-02-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-02-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/123-the-consumption-pyramid> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Frankly-123-Transcript-The-Consumption-Pyramid.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/EOmG0B2q9aI> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-the-consumption-pyramid> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:AnimatedVideo.Part2TheHumanSuperorganism, tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries, tgs:PersonalIntervention.LowerDopaminePeaks ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real site inconsistency worth flagging plainly, not silently accepted: this episode's own real page carries meta-description tags (used for search/social previews) describing an entirely different, unrelated episode about AI and biophysical limits — the real, correct 'Description' section further down the same page matches this episode's actual content and the uploaded transcript exactly, and that is what this build is based on. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Frankly #44 ('The Many Shapes of the Carbon Pulse,' already flagged deferred earlier this session), Frankly #122 ('A Country of Geniuses,' the immediately preceding episode), and Frankly #124 ('Wide Boundary News,' the immediately following episode and Nate's own real revised peak-oil call, already flagged deferred during the Frankly_154 build). Anna Lembke's real prior TGS episode (#206) is directly cited again, matching the same real episode number already flagged deferred during the Frankly_154 build. The 7-layer pyramid itself is genuinely rich, structured content — comparable in kind to the Frankly_58 archetype system and the Frankly_154 PersonalIntervention set, both already fully minted this session — but was NOT built out into individuals in this pass, since MJSullivan's request was to build the episode itself, not explicitly the taxonomy; captured here in prose instead, in the episode's own definition and discussion summary. Worth a dedicated follow-up pass if wanted, following the same pattern already established (thinkr:ConsumptionLayer as a new class, ordered 1-7, linked from this episode via a hasLayer-style property)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Grounded in an unusually candid, specific personal story: Nate discloses that the idea for this framework came to him during an overnight hospital stay for knee surgery, when a planned stop at a favorite Indian restaurant was thwarted by the restaurant being locked and gated — he later realized this coincided with real ICE activity staging nearby in Minneapolis, prompting a direct, uncomfortable reflection on how his own minor inconvenience (missing dinner) sat alongside potentially much higher-stakes disruption for the restaurant's own staff. Nate is careful and explicit that the pyramid is not a purity test: most people move across several layers within the same week, and he discloses his own real fluctuation directly ('I can have super discipline for three months straight and then slide upward in this pyramid during a stressful week, that's me too'). A real, structural nuance he insists on: the same physical object can sit on different layers for different people depending entirely on context — a smartphone as pure status object for one person, genuine functional necessity for another who depends on it for banking and public services. The framework's real stakes, per Nate's own direct argument, are asymmetric under contraction: higher layers depend on complex, brittle, global systems that don't degrade gracefully, while lower layers tend to be more local, repairable, and substitutable — making intentional descent, before conditions force it, a real form of what he calls sovereignty rather than austerity. The episode closes with a real, direct callback (John Merrick's 'I am not an animal, I am a human being') repurposed as Nate's own closing line — 'I am not a consumer, I am a human being' — followed by a real, concrete request that listeners share the framework with 2-3 others rather than simply consume it alone."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_124_WideBoundaryNews a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wide Boundary News: Peak Oil (Not!), Peak Dispatchability, and WEF Risks"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct self-correction opens this installment of Nate's Wide Boundary News series: Q4 2025 marked a genuine new all-time high in true crude-plus-condensate oil production, directly contradicting Nate's own earlier public prediction, stated with 90% confidence, that November 2018 would stand as the all-time peak. He's candid about the real, compound causes — debt-fueled drilling, AI-assisted exploration productivity, OPEC policy, and a lagged supply response to the 2022 Russia-Ukraine price spike — while naming the real Permian Basin's coming decline and a likely 'next great migration' to Alberta's Montney and Duvernay shales as the probable next chapter."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-02-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-02-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/124-wide-boundary-news> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_237_ArtBerman ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_l3n3_gR1xE> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Frankly-124-Transcript-Wide-Boundary-News-2_9_26.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.BiophysicalGauntlet ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_121_WideBoundaryNews ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct technical distinction anchors the electricity section: US 'dispatchable' power (Nate's own real, explicit shorthand for reliable 24/7 capacity, distinct from formal 'baseload') has been declining for nearly 20 years even as total generation capacity grows, since the real growth has come almost entirely from weather-dependent wind and solar — a genuine structural mismatch he connects directly to accelerating AI data-center demand, which requires firm, always-on power rather than average availability. The episode's real emotional center is an original, extended metaphor: Nate imagines the real World Economic Forum's Global Risks Perception Survey findings (near-term risks judged social/geopolitical, but the top 10-year risks all ecological) as an ornate executive conference table where biosphere representatives — 'the elephants, the oceans, the forests' — sit with hands patiently raised, never called on amid louder, more animated arguments over AI, debt, and war. He paraphrases (not quotes at length) a recurring maxim from Robin Hobb's Farseer trilogy — his own real, stated favorite book series, 'better than Tolkien even' — about never taking irreversible action without first weighing what can no longer be undone. The episode's most delicate real moment is handled with direct, deliberate restraint: the show's first-ever mention of Jeffrey Epstein, framed explicitly not as spectacle but as a real, direct cultural and institutional signal about dark-triad-shaped ascension to power (already discussed in this graph via RR-19) — Nate is careful to credit the real, specific mechanism that brought it to light ('a small group of humans, mostly the victims, standing up for what mattered'), and frames the moment as a real test of collective self-recognition rather than simple condemnation of individuals."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Frankly #121 ('Wide Boundary News: Japan, Silver, Venezuela, and More'), the real, inaugural episode of this same Wide Boundary News series and the real origin of the 'biophysical gauntlet' framing used again here, is now built (2026-08-31) and formally referenced above. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Art Berman (already a real, built Persona in this graph via multiple episodes) is directly re-cited via a real X/Twitter post, independently reconfirming his ongoing real relevance to this graph's own energy-systems content; Robin Hobb (real, directly named author, not built as a Persona, given no direct connection to Nate or the show beyond personal literary appreciation)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_125_UncomfortableQuestionsInUnstableTimes a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The End of 'Progress?': Uncomfortable Questions for Unsettled Times"@en ;
    skos:definition "Real, direct Part 1 of tgs:Series.UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimes, and its own real, direct origin episode -- Nate explicitly launches this as a new, real, recurring segment here, prompted directly by an Einstein line he cites ('the formulation of the problem is often more essential than its solution'). Seven real, direct questions, moving from growth-vs-stability as society's implicit default, through a real, direct preview of what becomes Frankly-126's own Jekyll-and-Hyde-at-scale framing, into three real, direct questions on AI specifically -- ending on tgs:Concept.PurityVsEffectiveness, Nate's own real, direct, unresolved worry about voluntary restraint becoming a coordination disadvantage against actors who won't restrain themselves."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-02-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-02-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimes ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 1 ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/125-uncomfortable-questions-in-unstable-times> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Frankly-125-Transcript-Uncomfortable-Questions-in-Unstable-Times.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.PurityVsEffectiveness, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_158_JohnVervaeke, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_112_TheQuadrupleBifurcation,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_125_VanessaAndreotti, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_10_TheEcologyOfCommunication,
        tgs:Persona.NancyMcWilliams, tgs:Persona.ReidMeloy ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct launch framing: Nate opens by citing Einstein directly ('the formulation of the problem is often more essential than its solution'), explicitly naming this as a new, real, recurring segment born from his own notebook filling up, comprised of five-to-ten unanswerable-but-not-rhetorical questions meant as conversation starters, not rhetoric. Real, direct theory-of-change framing: the platform's own real impact is argued to come less from information conveyed than from the two-way flow of ideas and response across the global TGS community -- directly announcing plans to hire a real Director of Community Engagement the same week. Real, direct question one: if a society quietly pivoted from growth to stability as its implicit default goal, what would change in one's own life, and what would one want protected first versus willing to let go. Real, direct question two, framed as a wide-boundary moral question: suffering under systemic strain is unevenly distributed by proximity to the edge, not by weakness, raising the question of responsibility toward people (and ecosystems) already experiencing the future earlier than oneself. Real, direct question three, on meaning specifically: comfortable-but-not-meaningful modern life is named directly as fertile ground for addiction, scapegoating, and authoritarian impulses, with meaning framed as a real, direct societal stabilizer, not merely a nice-to-have. Real, direct question four, explicitly previewing the real, upcoming Jekyll-and-Hyde-at-scale framing (see Frankly-126): citing a real, prior conversation with J. Reid Meloy and Nancy McWilliams on psychopathy and dark triad (RR-19), Nate argues scale itself changes incentives so that a small number of unusually forceful, strategic personalities can steer a nation's 'signal' far past their actual numbers -- applied directly to the US and China as real, live test cases, with the median/mean distinction reframing the question as one of citizen responsibility for a nation's loudest outputs, not blame. Real, direct questions five through seven, on AI specifically: the 'AI bifurcation' (people becoming genuinely fluent with these tools versus using them only superficially) previewed directly from Frankly-112's own real Quadruple Bifurcation; a real, direct prediction that AI will keep losing public approval even as its physical buildout continues, producing rising household utility bills tied to a technology many people resent, raising a real, direct question about the ethical allocation of scarce energy, water, and materials between AI and essential household use; and a real, direct, specific prediction of an 'AI winter' within two to three years -- not a capability collapse, but a valuation and hype reset as physical bottlenecks (copper, water, tantalum, lanthanum) fail to scale as fast as the financial story assumes. Real, direct closing pattern (see tgs:Concept.PurityVsEffectiveness): powerful, dual-use technologies (fossil fuels, the internet, now AI) get sorted into moral identities and tribal purity tests, but actors most committed to narrow-boundary accumulation will use every available tool regardless, meaning voluntary restraint by those trying to protect the web of life risks becoming a genuine coordination disadvantage rather than a virtue -- closing on Nate's own real, direct, unresolved question of where the line sits between integrity and self-disarmament."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct correction: the transcript's own auto-generated text renders J. Reid Meloy's name as 'Reed Malloy' -- corrected here to the real, confirmed, already-existing Human/Persona in this graph (tgs:Human.ReidMeloy), verified directly via his own real, established RR-19 appearance alongside Nancy McWilliams, exactly matching this episode's own real, direct description of that prior conversation. Real, direct coincidental numbering note: this episode's own real show notes link to 'TGS Episode 125' for Vanessa Andreotti (tgs:Interview.TGS_125_VanessaAndreotti) -- a real, entirely different, unrelated entity that merely happens to share the number 125 with this Frankly episode; not a duplicate or error, just a coincidence worth naming so it isn't mistaken for one later. Real, direct date correction from this build's own prior partial entry: the real yaml confirms recording Feb 10 and publication Feb 13, 2026 -- correcting an earlier, approximate 'Feb 20' estimate drawn from a web search result before this episode's own real source material was available."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_126_HumanityAsDrJekyllAndMrHyde a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Humanity as Dr. Jekyll & Mr. Hyde: The Symptoms, Patterns, and Drivers"@en ;
    skos:definition "An unusually long, systematically structured Frankly (Nate's own framing: 'foundational... therefore a long one'), separating the more-than-human predicament into three explicit layers: symptoms (the familiar list — global heating, biodiversity loss, exceeding seven of nine planetary boundaries, rising inequality alongside all-time stock market highs, geopolitical tension, attention fragmentation), the recurring systemic patterns beneath them, and — the episode's real focus — the deeper driving forces locking those patterns in place. Names six such drivers (power-law concentration/the Matthew Principle, tragedy-of-the-commons dynamics, time-discounting/short-termism, and a narrowing cultural definition of 'us' that excludes the natural world, among others), framing all of them not as moral failures but as ancient, once-adaptive human instincts that destabilize systems once scale exceeds a real inflection point — Dr. Jekyll at tribal scale, Mr. Hyde at civilizational scale. Explicitly triggered by 'the recent Jeffrey Epstein files' as a live, current example of dark-triad-shaped institutional capture."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-02-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-02-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/126-humanity-as-dr-jekyll-and-mr-hyde> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nCHra4-tFIw> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Frankly-126-Transcript-Humanity-as-Dr.-Jekyll-and-Mr.-Hyde.pdf> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-humanity-as-dr-jekyll-and-mr> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_14_JohnGowdy ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct personal detail grounds the tragedy-of-the-commons section: Nate names his own real PhD advisor, Robert Costanza, as the originator of the 'social trap' framing he builds on. The power-law concentration section is developed with real, concrete cross-domain examples before ever reaching human systems — large trees intercepting most forest sunlight, a small share of river channels carrying most water flow — building the Matthew Principle (initial advantage compounding through feedback, 'the rich getting richer') as a real, general pattern rather than a human-specific critique. Nate is careful and repeated in his own framing that none of this describes moral failure: 'Mr. Hyde is not evil... what happens when ancient instincts operate inside systems that move faster and reach farther than our moral intuitions were shaped for.' Real, concrete partial answers are named rather than left abstract — Mondragon Cooperative enterprises and commons-based resource management as existing examples of systems that keep 'ancestral Jekyll traits' intact even as they scale — though Nate is honest that he doesn't yet have a full answer for whether such models can generalize beyond the local. Closes not with resolution but a real, direct set of open questions for the audience, explicitly flagging this as the start of a year-long thread he intends to keep developing rather than a settled conclusion."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, valuable discoveries surfaced by this episode's own show notes, not built in this pass: Lisi Krall — previously known in this graph only as John Gowdy's real co-author on Ultrasocial, now confirmed to have her own real, dedicated TGS episode (#86); Luke Kemp — already tracked as one of 11 real repeat-guest coverage gaps in docs/repeat-guest-coverage.md (tgs-digest), now confirmed with TWO real episode numbers (#194 and #153) rather than the two previously recorded there, worth updating that tracker. Real, notable title discrepancy: this episode's own show notes cite Frankly_108 under the title 'Frankly: Why Humans Are Better Than We Think,' but this graph's existing stub for the same canonical URL uses 'The Influence of Psychopaths' (sourced from RR-26's own citation) — not resolved in this pass, worth checking against Frankly_108's own real page when it's eventually built out. dct:references to TGS_14 (John Gowdy) reflects this episode's own direct citation of his real work on the economic Superorganism; John Gowdy is himself already a real, built Persona and Episode in this graph."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_127_WideBoundaryNews a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wide Boundary News 2/23/26"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, structured 'denominator check' anchors this Wide Boundary News installment: wind and solar generated more EU electricity than fossil fuels for the first time (30% versus 29%), but Nate pairs this directly against real German de-industrialization data — BASF unprofitable in Germany for two years, over 100,000 manufacturing jobs lost, nearly 1,000 manufacturing bankruptcies in the first half of 2025, and five consecutive quarters of negative GDP — arguing the renewable share looks better partly because industrial demand, the denominator, is shrinking. Applies the identical real lens to China: 2025 CO2 emissions were flat to slightly down, but Nate traces this to a real construction-sector collapse (cement production down nearly 10%) masking record real coal production (4.8 billion tons) rather than genuine clean-energy displacement."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-02-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-02-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/127-wide-boundary-news> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.BiophysicalPyramid ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_21_ArmsRaceOrTheHumanRace, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_121_WideBoundaryNews, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_61_TheStraitOfHormuzAndTheSpice ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Introduces a real, structured three-pricing-model framework for comparing national energy strategies: Model 1 (unhindered cost pricing, e.g. China), Model 2 (adding a 'green wedge' for ecological externalities), and Model 3 (adding both a green wedge and a 'depletion wedge' taxing non-renewable inputs, e.g. Germany's roughly $10/gallon gasoline) — Nate's own real, direct argument is that Model 3 nations make the best long-term decisions while being systematically outcompeted short-term by Model 1 nations, since all three run simultaneously on an uneven playing field. A real, striking climate-attribution mechanism grounds the warming-record segment: decades of simultaneously rising greenhouse gases and rising sulfur-pollution aerosols (which reflect sunlight and brighten clouds) partially masked underlying warming — as sulfur pollution is now being cleaned up for real public-health reasons, that shading is dissipating and accumulated warming is becoming more fully visible. A real, striking and counterintuitive ecology finding closes the environmental section: a century of biodiversity survey data shows species turnover has significantly slowed rather than accelerated as ecologists predicted under warming — read not as stability but as ecosystems losing the internal 'spare parts' needed for future reorganization, a real, structural fragility that looks fine until sudden collapse. The New START nuclear treaty's real February 4, 2026 expiration without replacement — leaving zero legally binding limits on the US and Russian nuclear arsenals for the first time since the 1970s — is directly connected to the real RR-21 panel with Mark Medish and Chuck Watson (both already built in this graph), with Watson's own real concept of 'risk homeostasis' (behavioral desensitization to repeated safe outcomes, like habitually running red lights) applied directly to escalating Iran tensions and repeated past 'surgical' US military strikes. Closes on a real, pointed cultural observation: real-money prediction markets (Polymarket) offering live odds on whether the US will bomb Iran are read directly as evidence that geopolitical violence has been fully absorbed into the entertainment and wagering layer of culture, with the event's actual civilizational gravity and its casual, betting-app treatment now 'completely decoupled.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest transcription correction: the raw transcript renders 'Mark Medish' (RR-21's real co-panelist, already built in this graph) as 'Mark Menish' — corrected directly here after cross-referencing RR-21's own real guest list. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: the real Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists and Chatham House reports on 2026 nuclear escalation risk, both directly cited as institutional sources rather than individual citations."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_128_UltraProcessedInformation a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ultra-Processed Information: AI and the Coming Deluge of Noise"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly opens with a real, personal observation: opening his phone each morning increasingly produces disorientation rather than the clarity he's looking for, prompting a real, direct analogy between AI-generated information and industrially processed food — both made cheap and abundant by a fossil-fueled system while quietly stripping out what actually nourishes."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-02-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-02-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/128-ultra-processed-information> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ucgyd5ML0j4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Frankly-128-Transcript-Ultra-Processed-Information.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.UltraProcessedInformation, tgs:Concept.ThreeInformationFilters, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_20_HackingHumanAttachment, tgs:Interview.TGS_22_AzaRaskin, tgs:Human.TristanHarris ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly distinguishes two real, diverging outcomes as AI-generated content scales: for those with strong foundational knowledge and skill using large language models, AI can genuinely increase true signal, while for the majority, the sheer increase in content volume will far outpace any increase in real signal, risking a genuine feeling of drowning. He's real and direct that the coming glut isn't accidental: just as food manufacturers engineer a 'bliss point' of salt, sugar, and fat, AI content is becoming engineered for an attention-economy equivalent — novelty, polarization, and righteousness blended for maximum engagement rather than truth. A real, direct, uncomfortable prediction closes his own analogy: just as the physical obesity crisis was 'solved' not by fixing the food system but by overlaying it with profitable pharmaceutical appetite suppressants (GLP-1 drugs), he directly predicts any future 'cognitive GLP-1' will likely be sold by the very same companies whose AI systems created the underlying problem. He's real and direct that Zak Stein and Nora Bateson's own already-built Reality Roundtable discussion directly informs his concern about cognitive atrophy: as AI increasingly handles research, synthesis, and writing, the cognitive muscles once required for that effort begin to atrophy, compounding the disorientation from information overload itself. Closes on his own real, practical three-filter discipline — is it true, is it relevant, is it useful — directly crediting friends Aza Raskin and Tristan Harris (both already built) for the foundational observation that attention itself is becoming civilization's scarcest resource, closing with a real, direct, personally resonant line: 'in a world where words are about to become incredibly cheap, our careful attention may just become an expression of our ethics.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Robert Lustig's own real TGS-69 appearance ('Processed Food, Metabolism, and The Ills of Society'), directly credited as the source of the food-system half of this episode's own central analogy, not yet built; Tristan Harris's own real TGS-16 appearance, directly cited, not yet built despite Harris himself already being a built Human in this graph."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Guide to Staying Human (Part 1): Desperately Seeking Agency"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-24 from a stub, using the full real transcript and official show notes. Opens against the backdrop of the real 2026 Iran war / Strait of Hormuz crisis (Operation Epic Fury), but Nate explicitly declines geopolitical analysis to launch a new, tentatively-titled 'Staying Human' series instead, opening Part 1 with the real question: why does growing awareness of the more-than-human predicament so often produce paralysis rather than action? He traces the gap through learned-helplessness and self-efficacy research (nervous systems learning whether effort produces outcomes), frames a digital environment designed to fragment attention as actively degrading follow-through, and reframes the Superorganism (already modeled in this graph) as a system that advertises choice while eroding the conditions for it. Closes with a concrete practice, not a program: protect one real hour in the next three days as a kept promise to rebuild self-trust — the precondition Nate argues is required before community-level and institutional work can follow."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.AGuideToStayingHuman ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 1 ;
    dct:created "2026-03-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/129-a-guide-to-staying-human-part-1> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_159_TheRealWork ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Frankly-129-Transcript-A-Guide-to-Staying-Human-Part-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ao2MJeiPiL4>, <https://open.spotify.com/episode/0WdRQsyD7koPMV6i4tFNvS> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-a-guide-to-staying-human-part> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.TheoryOfChange, tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: three real cross-referenced episodes in this episode's own show notes — Frankly #61 ('The Strait of Hormuz and the Spice'), Frankly #127 ('Wide Boundary News 2/23/26'), and Frankly #98 ('10 Qualities That Could Change the Future: The Seeds of New Cultural Mitochondria', also cited by Frankly_132) — none built as Episode individuals yet. Real, well-evidenced NEW Concept candidates surfaced but not minted this pass: Learned Helplessness and Self-Efficacy (Bandura) — cited only in this episode so far, held pending recurrence elsewhere, unlike Concept.PersonalAgency which this series' own Part 2 directly builds on and cross-references."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_130_WideBoundaryNews31026 a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Good morning. It is Tuesday, March 10th, 7:30 AM Central. I recorded the following video yesterday morning about the wide boundary risks of the unfolding Iran war. My team worked very hard to get it ready to release yesterday, and then oil fell over $30 a barrel, and President Trump announced a press conference last night. So I delayed its release in case something dramatic wasn't announced. It wasn't, but this situation will likely be a rollercoaster for a while, and the news and the markets may stay disconnected from real events on the ground. Either way, the global system has now fundamentally changed as a result of the events in Iran. So the points that follow will stay relevant no matter what happens, and The Great Simplification has likely gotten closer. Here's yesterday's video."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wide Boundary News: The Iranian War, Rising Gas Prices, and the Single Point Failure"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct Wide Boundary News installment (real number 130, unnumbered on the site itself -- confirmed by MJSullivan directly, per this episode's own real yaml resolution notes), recorded during the real, active early days of the 2026 Iran war and the effective closure of the Strait of Hormuz. Real, direct core reframe: oil registers as only roughly 3% of global GDP by cost, but underpins effectively 100% of economic activity, since modern life is a continuous conversion of fuel into food, heat, motion, and materials. Traces a real, direct cascade of hidden second- and third-order dependencies -- sulfur, LNG, nitrogen fertilizer -- before closing on a real, direct three-way religious-eschatology analysis of the conflict and a real, direct civilizational warning invoking the War of 1812."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/wide-boundary-news-3-10-26> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Frankly-130-Transcript-WBN-10-March-2026.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.RocksInTheRiver, tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s, tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_61_TheStraitOfHormuzAndTheSpice, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_127_WideBoundaryNews,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_88_SnowSingularityRocksInTheRiver, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_98_TenQualitiesThatCouldChangeTheFuture,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_207_CraigTindale ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct scale-correction opens the substantive content: energy is only about 3% of global GDP by cost, but civilization doesn't run on financial capital or ingenuity alone -- it runs on those things coupled with a continuous flow of dense, portable, storable, inexpensive energy, so losing that flow risks nearly all of GDP, not a few percentage points of it. Real, direct hidden-dependency cascade, presented as the episode's own central example: sour crude passing through Hormuz yields sulfur as a byproduct; sulfur is the feedstock for sulfuric acid; sulfuric acid leaches copper and cobalt from the ground in the DRC and Zambia (over a sixth of global copper, over 70% of global cobalt); losing that chain means no transformers, no grid expansion, no data centers, no EV charging, no AI buildout -- a real, direct chain with 'very little to do with gasoline prices.' Real, direct parallel dependencies named: Qatar supplies roughly 20% of globally-traded LNG with no overland alternative; over 40% of internationally-traded nitrogen fertilizer originates from or transits the Gulf, risking rapid food-price inflation in import-dependent, fiscally thin nations like Egypt, Pakistan, and Turkey -- directly citing guest Craig Tindale's own real 'globalized Arab Spring' framing. Real, direct military stock-and-flow argument: the US and Israel have reportedly fired 5-7 interceptor missiles (roughly $4 million each) per incoming Iranian missile, against Shahed drones costing roughly $50,000 -- a real, direct 100:1 cost-exchange ratio favoring Iran, meaning Iran doesn't need to win outright, only sustain intermittent launches long enough to exhaust US/Israeli stocks. Real, direct, explicitly-labeled-speculative reservoir-damage scenario: Iraq's aging, water-injected oil fields may suffer permanent, unfixable damage if shut in during prolonged closure, versus the ecologically catastrophic but reservoir-preserving alternative of flaring or dumping crude -- Nate's own real, direct 'uncomfortable system logic' framing of why the dump option may look rational the shorter the closure is expected to last. Real, direct editorial opinion, explicitly flagged as such: political obsession with cheap gas prices is one of the most ecologically and economically counterproductive reflexes in modern governance, since cheap prices falsely signal abundance, sustainability, and low urgency for alternatives, when the truth is depletion of a stock, not a flow. Real, direct three-way eschatology analysis, presented as the wide-boundary story mainstream coverage misses entirely: US troops reporting commanders framing the war as fulfilling Christian Armageddon prophecy (200+ complaints across 30+ installations per the Military Religious Freedom Foundation); a Jerusalem Post rabbi connecting the war to Ezekiel's Gog and Magog prophecy and the Messiah's arrival; Iranian IRGC framing their own Supreme Leader's death as martyrdom advancing the Twelfth Imam's prophesied return -- Nate's own real, direct concern being who is actively seeking off-ramps when all sides believe God is directing events. Real, direct closing civilizational warning, invoking the War of 1812 (the US assuming a quick, decisive war, instead nearly losing the capital and the country) as historical precedent for how badly a confident military miscalculation can go. Real, direct closing pivot, matching Frankly-88's own real 'rocks in the river' metaphor directly: the platform's own real focus is shifting from further wide-boundary commentary toward local coordination and ecological preparedness -- 'putting rocks in the river, in communities, so that we can slow the current and start rebuilding capacity.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct minting note: tgs:Concept.RocksInTheRiver is built here, grounded in Frankly-88 (its real origin, already confirmed via RR-26's own independent citation), given this is now the second real episode in this session's own build queue to cite it directly and substantively -- crossing the threshold for a formal Concept rather than remaining prose-only. Real, direct numbering note preserved from the source yaml: this episode is unnumbered on the show's own official site; '130' comes from the filename alone, confirmed directly by MJSullivan (2026-08-21), distinct from the numbered Wide Boundary News installments (121, 124, 127, 141) that do carry official site numbers."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_131_IranEffectsLocalPreparednessAndEndOfEmpire a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Good morning. I am freshly showered and ready to have a three hour podcast on thermodynamics and civilization with Tad Patek, and I have a few minutes to record a timely, Frankly, on uncomfortable questions."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Preparing for a More Fragile World: Iran Effects and End of Empire?"@en ;
    skos:definition "Real, direct Part 2 of 'Uncomfortable Questions for Unsettled Times' (see tgs:Series.UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimes), recorded the same real morning as Nate's own real, direct TGS-219 conversation with Tad Patzek. Real, direct five-question structure, moving from the practical to the interior: what's genuinely essential versus merely familiar in modern supply-chain-dependent life; what assigning blame actually does to the nervous system and capacity for response; whether the collapse of US global power would be net positive or negative for the world; imagining one's own town in 2050 and what to begin now with a few others; and fear, drawing directly on Dune's Litany Against Fear, as a force that narrows perception and shrinks the capacity to act."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimes ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 2 ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/131-iran-effects-local-preparedness-and-end-of-empire> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Frankly-131-Transcript-Uncomfortable-Questions-in-Unsettled-Times.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_219_TadPatzek, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_126_HumanityAsDrJekyllAndMrHyde,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_130_WideBoundaryNews31026, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_61_TheStraitOfHormuzAndTheSpice ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening context: recorded the same morning as a real, planned three-hour conversation with Tad Patzek on thermodynamics and civilization, prompted by real, positive listener feedback (including photos of real, in-person group discussions) after the first Uncomfortable Questions installment. Real, direct question one: modern supply-chain dependence has made highly complex, temporary arrangements feel permanently normal -- asks directly what three things a person could not do without if global supply chains simplified quickly, and what three things they could voluntarily reduce now before scarcity forces the choice. Real, direct question two, building on Frankly-126's own real Jekyll-and-Hyde systems framing: assigning blame is an evolutionarily understandable response to hardship, but Nate's own real, direct question is what the act of blame-assignment does to one's own nervous system and capacity for response -- does it increase real agency, or mostly give shape to existing distress. Real, direct question three, explicitly named as harder and more important than the moralizing version of the same topic: would the collapse of US global power be net positive or negative for the world, materially and systemically over decades, not just satisfying in the short run -- with a real, direct challenge to whoever answers either way to specify what would fill the resulting power vacuum and why it would be better. Real, direct question four: imagining one's own real town, watershed, or neighborhood in 2050 under best-, worst-, and likely-case scenarios, then backcasting to name two or three real, concrete things a small group could begin now to nudge toward the better end of that distribution -- Nate's own real, honest admission that he hasn't done as much of this locally as he thinks he should, committing directly to doing more. Real, direct question five, grounded in Dune's real Litany Against Fear ('fear is the mind-killer'): fear narrows perceptual aperture and shrinks the felt availability of present-moment action -- Nate's own real, honest disclosure that his own first reaction to the weekend's Hormuz escalation was emotional, not the detached analysis of Tuesday's Wide Boundary News, though he notes with some pride that fear didn't drive him to panic-buying. Real, direct closing framing: better questions are argued to matter as much as more information, since they're themselves a form of preparedness, clarifying what's valued, depended on, and who one may need to become."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct series confirmation and formalization (2026-09-16): this episode's own real opening line -- 'the first uncomfortable questions episode was about our general trajectory, and this one... is more immediate' -- confirms it as Part 2, alongside Part 3 (Frankly-134) and Part 4 (Frankly-146), both already built earlier this session. tgs:Series.UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimes is minted here for the first time, now that three of at least four real parts are confirmed; Frankly-134 and Frankly-146 were each updated with formal dct:isPartOf/seriesPosition properties to match, replacing their own prior prose-only series mentions. Part 1 remains genuinely unconfirmed. Real, direct show notes discrepancy worth flagging: this episode's own real yaml self-reports 'weak confidence' on its own URL resolution, since the site's real slug doesn't closely resemble the uploaded filename -- noted directly, not silently resolved."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "What to Do as the World Falls Apart: A Framework for Action"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-13 from a stub, using the full real transcript and official show notes. MJSullivan flagged this as a second key episode this session (alongside Frankly_139), 'as it provides an actionable framework for things you can do.' Recorded during the real 2026 Iran war/Strait of Hormuz crisis, Nate explicitly marks this as a turning point — after 20+ years of diagnosis, he shifts to a first-pass, explicitly labeled draft framework for action, organized as 4 levels: a personal foundation of inner work (nervous-system regulation, agency, grief work), 'Finding the Others' (trusted networks, shared vocabulary, scenario/shortfall planning), Six Fronts of intervention (Infrastructure & physical stock/flow, Poverty & displacement, Ecological interventions, Civic resilience & governance, Culture & meaning, Economic transition/commons/post-growth), and a Timeline axis of three overlapping, non-sequential phases (Phase A: the Stability Window; Phase B: Bend Not Break; Phase C: the Stable Attractor) that cut across all six fronts."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:created "2026-03-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/132-what-to-do-as-the-world-falls-apart> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Frankly-132-Transcript-What-to-Do-as-the-World-Falls-Apart.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/TH8aHQ3Y6Zk> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-what-to-do-as-the-world-falls> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency, tgs:Concept.CulturalMitochondria, tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency,
        tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_23_ThePlasticDetox, tgs:Persona.ShannaSwan, tgs:Persona.SianSutherland,
        tgs:Persona.ZakStein, tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_58_FellowshipBendNotBreak,
        <https://ctb.ku.edu/en/creating-and-maintaining-coalitions-and-partnerships> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct addition (2026-09-13): added a real, external reference -- the University of Kansas Community Tool Box's own real, practical guide, \"Creating and Maintaining Coalitions and Partnerships\" -- directly supporting Step 2's own real content (finding the others, building capacity, constituency mapping) with established, actionable, real-world guidance beyond what Nate himself provides in the transcript. Also added: a real, direct link to The Great Simplification's own existing Hylo community group -- a genuine, practical starting point for readers who don't yet know who their own \"others\" are. Also, real, direct gap closed (2026-09-13): this episode itself was missing thinkr:discusses links to tgs:Concept.CulturalMitochondria and tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence, despite Nate directly using both terms by name in the real transcript ('seeding the cultural mitochondria,' 'islands of coherence') -- caught only once the full transcript was reviewed directly for a WWNS article, not during the original build. Both concepts are already real, confirmed, built individuals (Frankly_98 carries both; IslandsOfCoherence also appears on Frankly_114 and TGS_59_JonathanHaidt). tgs:Concept.RocksInTheRiver, the third term Nate uses in this same passage, remains genuinely unbuilt as a Concept -- distinct from the other two, not yet minted at all, not just unlinked here. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Frankly_88 ('Snow, the Singularity, and Rocks in the River') and Frankly_65 ('And Then What, Using Wide Boundary Lenses') — real, confirmed URLs, direct real sources for Rocks in the River specifically, still unbuilt. Audrey Tang (TGS #169) and Helena Norberg-Hodge (TGS #181) both real, confirmed, cited by name with direct episode links — neither built as a Persona in this pass. Peter Strack (TGS #166, 2000-Watt Society) and Alexis Zeigler (TGS #171, Living Energy Farm) both real, confirmed, cited — neither built. TWO items this note previously flagged as deferred were resolved on 2026-08-13, the same day as the original build: Frankly_58 ('The Fellowship of the Ring, Bend Not Break') was built as a real Episode individual and added to dct:references above; and the SIX FRONTS/30 SUBDOMAINS/THREE TIMELINE PHASES this episode introduces were fully minted (tgs:Concept.FrameworkForAction, tgs:InterventionFront, tgs:InterventionSubdomain, tgs:ResponsePhase — see interventionfronts.ttl and responsephases.ttl)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate frames this explicitly and repeatedly as a personal turning point, not just another episode — 'as much a personal rite of passage as it is a Frankly,' gestating for a decade and finally organized under the pressure of real, live world events (the Iran war, unfolding in real time as he records). A genuinely notable rhetorical move: Nate directly names the risk of his own prior 20 years of work — that continued diagnosis without a action framework becomes 'a category error' once the underlying diagnosis is broadly accepted — and explicitly declines to spend this episode on news updates about the Strait of Hormuz crisis itself, redirecting instead to what he calls the only proportionate conversation: what to actually do. The most philosophically pointed moment is Nate's direct, carefully-hedged engagement with anti-capitalist and decolonial critique: he states real sympathy for those values and concedes 'there's much in those critiques that's correct,' but argues explicitly that naming the system correctly is not itself a plan, and that waiting for total systemic transformation as a precondition for any other action means 'waiting forever' — arguing instead for parallel construction, building new structures while the old ones still stand. The Timeline axis is offered as the episode's own 'most important orienting idea' — explicitly not a sequence to complete and move past, but three genuinely overlapping conditions, with Phase C (the stable attractor) framed as impossible to reach by skipping the disruption of Phases A and B, only reachable by building through them. The episode closes on a real, personal reflection on leadership: Nate argues modern systems select for Dark Triad traits (already a real, built Concept in this graph) in leaders, creating adverse selection, and offers a genuinely counter-intuitive closing reframe — that a viewer's own reluctance to lead 'might be a good indication that you could be a good leader tomorrow.' He explicitly declines to resolve the framework's own open question of what to do, insisting that question 'can only be answered by us as individuals,' a real refusal to prescribe despite having just spent nearly an hour building a framework that could easily be read as prescriptive."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_133_IranUsAndTheRest a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Iran, U.S., and the Rest: The Unavoidable Pig in the Python"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, deliberately unpolished personal reflection, recorded during the real, early days of the 2026 Iran war, on the real, uneven, lagged global impact of fossil-fuel disruption -- the US, and especially energy-independent parts of it, relatively insulated, versus the roughly three-quarters of the world's population living in net fossil-fuel-importing countries already rationing fuel and electricity. Real, direct central argument: financial markets are only historically and short-run correlated with biophysical reality, and that correlation is now reversing as 'atoms and joules and handshakes' recede -- closing on two real, contrasting listener stories (a Lebanese meditation teacher whose olive farm was bombed; a Bali resident grateful for a news-disconnected local community) as direct, lived evidence of how proximity shapes response to the more-than-human predicament."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/133-iran-us-and-the-rest> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Frankly-133-Transcript-Iran-U.S.-and-the-Rest.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.PredicamentNotProblem, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.MarketReflexivity ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_121_WideBoundaryNews, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction, tgs:Human.GeorgeSoros ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening self-framing: Nate names himself not only as analyst and podcaster but 'a brother, a son, a partner, a friend, a human,' explicitly signaling this episode carries what he feels in his body, not just his analysis. Real, direct predicament/problem distinction stated plainly (see tgs:Concept.PredicamentNotProblem): a problem has solutions, a predicament only has pathways that are better or worse, and the current default pathway looks 'pretty dark.' Real, direct global-exposure argument: the US is largely energy independent but California specifically, an 'energy island' dependent on Asian refined-product imports, faces real, direct $10/gallon gas risk; globally, three-quarters of the world's population lives in net fossil-fuel-importing countries, over 50 countries import more than half their energy, and a fifth of global GDP comes from countries getting over two-thirds of their energy from imports -- Japan alone drawing nearly three-quarters of its oil through the Strait of Hormuz. Real, direct, already-unfolding second-order effects cited by name: the Philippines declaring a national energy emergency, Bangladesh and Thailand closing universities and ordering stairs over elevators, South Korea urging shorter showers, multiple African nations already rationing fuel. Real, direct financial-reality argument, crediting George Soros's own real reflexivity theory by name: the belief that markets steer reality is 'historically and in the very short run, something like 90% true,' but as physical reality reasserts itself, that correlation doesn't just shrink, it likely reverses to negative. Real, direct consumption-blindness admission: the average American consumes roughly 150 times the physical goods and services of the average human two centuries ago, entirely invisible in daily experience. Real, direct closing pair of listener stories, offered without forcing a single lesson: a Lebanese Mahamudra meditation teacher whose ancient olive farm was bombed, now urgently advising her own peers worldwide to adopt a grounding practice; a Bali resident overlooking rice fields, grateful for a local community of elders who don't follow the news and instead share simple work and stories -- held up directly against 'some of my American friends' treating the war as background noise, still gaming market moves or watching basketball brackets. Real, direct honest admission closing the episode: Nate says plainly he doesn't know what to say that would be both honest and make anyone feel better, other than that no one is alone in waking up to the systemic situation -- and names time itself as perhaps the most valuable, fastest-depleting resource, asking listeners directly to reflect on how they're using theirs. Real, direct forward-reference: mentions having presented 'a draft framework for response' the prior week, already revised, with more to come -- almost certainly the earliest real precursor to what becomes Frankly-132's own fully-developed Framework for Action."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct forward-reference worth flagging: this episode's own real show notes link directly to Frankly-132 ('What to Do as the World Falls Apart'), despite Frankly-132 being recorded and published months later -- confirming the show notes for this episode were updated retroactively after Frankly-132's own release, not that episode numbering reflects a different chronology than recording dates. Real, direct update (2026-09-16): George Soros and his own real reflexivity theory are now built (tgs:Human.GeorgeSoros, tgs:Concept.MarketReflexivity), scoped specifically to the theory directly credited here -- not a full account of his own extensive, separate political and philanthropic activities, which fall outside this episode's own real, direct citation."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_134_AWorldAtTheEdgeOfChange a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "What Does \"Winning\" Even Mean?: A World at the Edge of Change"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct Part 3 in Nate's 'Uncomfortable Questions for Unsettled Times' series (Part 4 is Frankly-146), recorded during the real, unfolding 2026 Iran war. Real, direct central three-part question: could the US and Israel actually lose this war, would tactical nuclear weapons be used against Iran's underground sites if not, and how does the US even define winning in the first place. Widens into a real, direct proposal that the global economic Superorganism might 'calve' into two competing East/West organisms, and closes on a real, direct contrast between individual/community responses to energy disruption (simplify, downsize, connect) and national responses (drill more, hoard, project more force)."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-04-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-03"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimes ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 3 ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/134-a-world-at-the-edge-of-change> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Frankly-134-Transcript-A-World-at-the-Edge-of-Change.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_146_UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimesPart4, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_127_WideBoundaryNews,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_121_WideBoundaryNews, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_61_TheStraitOfHormuzAndTheSpice,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_135_OilOneOhOne,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_136_OilTwoOhOne, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_137_OilThreeOhOne ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening barrage of real, unanswered practical questions -- well-damage from shut-in production, desalination risk to roughly 100 million people, insurance exposure at 'AIG 2008' scale -- before Nate names the episode's own real, central three-part question: could the US and Israel actually lose this war (citing real, direct evidence: an E-3 AWACS radar destroyed, interceptor missiles running low, Iran being 4x Iraq's size and population with fundamentally different drone technology than 2003); if not, would tactical nuclear weapons be used against Iran's deep underground sites; and if a nuclear taboo unbroken since 1945 were broken, how does the US even define 'winning' in the first place, given the White House, Pentagon, Wall Street, and deployed soldiers' families would each answer that differently. Real, direct trust-depletion argument: Oman had reportedly brokered a deal before bombing started anyway, and the US had already withdrawn from the JCPOA and struck Iran twice during active negotiations -- Nate's own real, direct question, borrowing a real Russian diplomatic term, is at what point the US becomes 'agreement incapable' in the eyes of the rest of the world, concluding 'trust is depleting faster than oil.' Real, direct central metaphor, extending Nate's own real economic-Superorganism framework: could this crisis cause the Superorganism to 'calve' into two competing East/West organisms, citing real, direct evidence already visible -- Russia feeding Iran satellite targeting data, China restricting fertilizer exports to stockpile domestically, Iran running a de facto two-tier Hormuz passage system, and real SWIFT alternatives being built. Real, direct climate irony raised directly: might the Iran war function as an accidental carbon tax through demand destruction, or trigger a carbon spike as nations revert to coal, or drive Haiti-style deforestation in poorer nations squeezed out of fertilizer and fuel markets. Real, direct 'Four Horsemen of the 2020s' framework named explicitly (see tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s), illustrated with a real, direct example of hidden complexity risk: a single black-paint shortage after the Fukushima earthquake shut down an entire Ford manufacturing plant -- Nate's own real, direct question is what the Hormuz crisis's own version of 'black paint' might turn out to be. Real, direct closing framing: this is either a dress rehearsal or the real thing, and either way asks the reader directly what they're doing today that they weren't doing a month ago -- with a real, direct, honest observed asymmetry: individuals and communities facing energy disruption tend to simplify and connect, while nations facing the same disruption tend to drill more, hoard, and project more force, and Nate poses directly which of those two opposite responses will end up shaping the other more."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct series confirmation, now fully resolved: this is Part 3 of 'Uncomfortable Questions for Unsettled Times,' with Part 4 confirmed as Frankly-146 (already built, cross-referencing this same episode). Not yet minted as a formal thinkr:Series individual -- tgs:Series.UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimes is used directly above as the expected real IRI, but Parts 1 and 2 remain unconfirmed; the Series individual itself should be built once those are found, following the same pattern as tgs:Series.OilTrilogy and tgs:Series.BendNotBreak. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: John Michael Greer (Collapse Now and Avoid the Rush, directly credited by name and title) has no existing entry in this graph -- a genuine candidate for a future Human/Work build."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_135_OilOneOhOne a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-10"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-10"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Untold Energy Story: What You Actually Need to Know About Oil"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly opens by naming the real, urgent occasion for this episode: with his team's own longer, professionally-produced 'Reality 101' series still in production, the real, unfolding Strait of Hormuz crisis prompted him to rush-produce this three-part video series on oil in three days. The first installment covers real, foundational concepts — what oil physically is, the real, direct work it performs, and why most people remain 'energy blind' to it."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/135-oil-101> ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.OilTrilogy ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 1 ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uRIxJ4B3fNQ> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Frankly-135-Transcript-Oil-101.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_136_OilTwoOhOne, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_77_SevenPhilosophiesOnTheFuture ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly reframes oil's own real origin story away from the popular 'dinosaur goo' image: it formed from ancient marine phytoplankton compressed over geologic time, a real 'solar battery that took millions of years to charge' and that humanity is draining in centuries. He's real and direct with the precise, quantified math: one barrel of oil contains roughly 1,700 kilowatt-hours of real work potential, equivalent to about 5 years of average human labor once combined with machines, at a real, current market cost of roughly $100 — an economic subsidy he calls 'indistinguishable from magic on human timescales.' Scaling this up, he's direct that roughly 100 billion barrels of oil-equivalent fossil hydrocarbons are consumed globally each year, equivalent to a real 500 billion human-labor-equivalents working alongside an actual 5 billion human workforce — a ratio of 100 fossil 'ghost workers' for every living one. He's real and direct about his own personal, professional experience of energy blindness: his three real, Nobel-Prize-winning graduate economics professors at the University of Chicago never once mentioned energy as a contributor to economic productivity, since the ample supply throughout their own careers let them treat a genuinely exceptional period of history as though it were simply normal. Closes on a real, direct, personal metabolic comparison: the human body requires roughly 2,000 kilocalories daily, while the average American's real, total energy footprint — heating, transport, food, supply chains — runs to roughly 200,000 kilocalories daily, a hundred times the body's own actual need, almost entirely invisible in daily experience."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct first installment of a genuine three-part trilogy, recorded back-to-back with Frankly_136 and Frankly_137 (both already built) and released on consecutive days. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic and institutional (EIA data, Reality Blind citations, real-time Strait of Hormuz news coverage) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_136_OilTwoOhOne a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Built for Cheap Energy: What Happens When the Oil Stops Flowing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The real, direct second installment of the Oil trilogy, extending the first video's own foundational framing into how modern institutions, food systems, and global supply chains are structurally calibrated to cheap, abundant energy — and what genuinely breaks when that assumption fails."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/136-oil-201> ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.OilTrilogy ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 2 ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_kEwDNlNwF0> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Frankly-136-Transcript-Oil-201.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_135_OilOneOhOne, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_137_OilThreeOhOne, tgs:Interview.TGS_101_ShaleOilAndTheSlurpingSound ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly grounds the food system's own real energy dependence in precise figures: roughly 10 calories of fossil hydrocarbon input go into every single calorie of food reaching a plate, and roughly half the nitrogen in a human body today carries a real, direct chemical signature traceable to the Haber-Bosch industrial process — a single process that alone enables Earth to feed roughly 4 of its 8 billion people. He's real and direct that swapping to electric vehicles addresses only a real, narrow slice of oil's total function: only about 40% of a barrel becomes gasoline, with the remainder feeding roughly 6,000 other real, everyday products, from asphalt to medicines to synthetic clothing. A real, pointed corrective closes his own treatment of corporate blame: only around 12% of global oil reserves belong to publicly traded companies like ExxonMobil or Shell, with the other 88% held by national oil companies — meaning oil is genuinely 'a story of nations and geology, not corporations.' He's real and direct in distinguishing energy from power: organisms and economies alike compete on power (the rate of usable energy delivery), not raw energy quantity, which is why oil's own real, direct combination of density, portability, and storability cannot simply be substituted by intermittent wind and solar without also solving for reliable, on-demand delivery. Closes on real, direct historical correction: contrary to the popular 'energy transition' narrative, humanity has never actually transitioned off a prior energy source, only ever added new ones on top — directly invoking Jevons paradox (efficiency gains expanding total consumption, not shrinking it) as the real, underlying mechanical reason why."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct second installment of the Oil trilogy — see Frankly_135 (already built) for the real, shared production context. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic and institutional (USDA dairy data, Exxon depletion figures, USGS critical-minerals data) rather than pointing to further individuals; TGS_101 (Art Berman, already built) is directly cross-referenced via its own real 'slurping sound' framing, not a new find."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_137_OilThreeOhOne a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Everything is About to Change: The World After Cheap Energy"@en ;
    skos:definition "The real, direct third and final installment of the Oil trilogy, framing the entire series through the Carbon Pulse: a one-time inheritance of ancient stored sunlight — real, direct source of the 'fossil sunlight' term — being burned through in a few hundred years, with direct, explicit implications for money, governance, ecology, and human psychology on its downslope."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/137-oil-301> ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.OilTrilogy ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 3 ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_Jv09GZ4lmE> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Frankly-137-Transcript-Oil-301.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_136_OilTwoOhOne, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_121_WideBoundaryNews, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_23_MordorEconomy, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly names crude oil and gas as 'fossil sunlight' — ancient solar energy captured by long-dead phytoplankton, concentrated by geologic time — closing the trilogy's own real, central metaphor. He's real and direct that money is a claim on physical work, not a substitute for it, arguing the entire growth-based financial system, its debt, interest, and repayment structures, is predicated on an assumption of ever-more affordable energy that the Carbon Pulse's own real shape does not actually support going forward. A real, direct historical grounding anchors his own warning about deforestation risk: citing Easter Island, the 19th-century US Eastern Seaboard, and Greece after its 2009 financial crisis as real, concrete precedents for what happens when populations lose access to their primary energy source and turn to wood instead. He's real and direct that the energy crisis and the ecological crisis are not two separate problems but 'two faces of the same predicament' — the Carbon Pulse simultaneously created the ecological damage from burning fossil fuels and the depletion crisis from extracting them too quickly. A real, pointed geopolitical observation closes his own systemic analysis: nations are now choosing sides based on energy access rather than ideology, directly citing China's real, long-term LNG and oil contracts with Qatar, Iran, and Russia as concrete evidence the post-WWII global order, itself built on cheap oil tied to American power, is now genuinely in question. Closes on real, direct personal conviction rather than pure systems analysis: naming connection, purpose, community, and service as 'ancient satisfactions' that predate the Carbon Pulse by hundreds of thousands of years and require no barrel of oil at all, framing the Great Simplification as a real, potential 'species growing up' rather than necessarily a collapse, and previewing a real, direct future series called 'Staying Human.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct source of the already-confirmed 'fossil sunlight' citation for tgs:Concept.CrudeOil's own alternate-term entry. Real, direct third installment of the Oil trilogy — see Frankly_135 and Frankly_136 (both already built) for the real, shared production context and Strait of Hormuz occasion. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely current-events/institutional (real-time 2026 Reuters/UNCTAD coverage of the Hormuz crisis, OPEC data) rather than pointing to further individuals; Frankly_75 ('Somebody's Gonna Win'), directly cited, is not yet built."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_138_Part1ChangingTheFuture a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "How to Think About the Future (Part 1): Changing the Future Starts with How You Think"@en ;
    skos:definition "Opens the series by naming a pattern Hagens says he hears repeatedly from the audience: people who have already settled on one storyline about the future and filter everything else through it. Introduces Scenario Thinking as the alternative, names 3 reasons holding multiple futures at once is hard in practice (the nervous system wants resolution, careers/identities attach to particular futures, cultural incentives reward confident stories over honest uncertainty), and closes by introducing Shortfall Risk, which the episode's own description states will act as connective tissue across the rest of the series."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-04-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.HowToThinkAboutTheFuture ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 1 ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/138-how-to-think-about-the-future-part-1> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/vgq_6GIHmCA> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Frankly-138-Transcript-How-to-Think-About-the-Future-Part-1.pdf> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-how-to-think-about-the-future> ;
    thinkr:discusses
        tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification,
        tgs:Concept.Wetware,
        tgs:Concept.TerrorManagementTheory,
        tgs:Concept.ShortfallRisk,
        tgs:Concept.ScenarioThinking,
        tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament,
        tgs:Concept.NarrativeAsActiveInference,
        tgs:Concept.Rebuildables,
        tgs:Concept.ComplexSystemsThinking ;
    dct:references
        tgs:Organization.TheOilDrum,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_85_WisdomNatureAndTheBrain,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_165_WisdomOverPower,
        tgs:Work.ComplexityAndPostmodernism,
        tgs:Work.GlobalTippingPointsReport2025 ;
    skos:scopeNote "REBUILT 2026-08-13 from Frankly_138's own official show-notes page (thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/138-...) — a real, structured, WordPress-generated per-episode topic index Hagens' team maintains, discovered this session as a much stronger extraction source than transcript-reading or generic NER. Every thinkr:discusses/dct:references link above traces to a specific labeled entry in that page, not inferred. Fixed 2 real bugs from this stub's first draft: thinkr:hasCanonicalSource was used twice (its own scopeNote documents cardinality-one; the essay link now correctly uses the general dct:source instead) and was typed as an xsd:anyURI string literal rather than a proper linked resource (thinkr:hasCanonicalSource is declared owl:ObjectProperty, not a datatype property — every other use of it in this graph uses a bare IRI)."@en .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_139_Part2FourVariables a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "How to Think About the Future (Part 2): Four Variables Shaping the Coming Decades"@en ;
    skos:definition "2ND PASS 2026-08-13, using the full real transcript (previously built from show notes only). This is the episode that formally introduces the complete 4-Dimension/16-Facet scenario framework this graph's own ScenarioDimension/ScenarioFacet structure is built from — Nate treats his long-standing 4-scenario Economic grid (Green Growth, Mordor, The Great Simplification, Mad Max) as the foundation, then layers three more grids on top: Power and Distribution (concentration of decision-making authority vs. where material gains flow), Geopolitical (cooperation vs. adversarial relations, crossed with interdependence vs. self-sufficiency), and Earth Systems (climate stress vs. biosphere integrity, explicitly from an already-compromised 2026 baseline, not a neutral one). Technology and demographics are both explicitly considered and deliberately NOT given their own grids — Nate argues both act as modifiers/amplifiers of whatever direction the underlying system is already moving, rather than independent dimensions."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.HowToThinkAboutTheFuture ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 2 ;
    dct:created "2026-04-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/139-how-to-think-about-the-future-part-2> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Frankly-139-Transcript-How-to-Think-About-the-Future-Part-2.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/5R0oUGPf9C4> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-how-to-think-about-the-future-92e> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism,
        tgs:ScenarioFacet.GreenGrowth, tgs:ScenarioFacet.Mordor, tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheGreatSimplification, tgs:ScenarioFacet.MadMax,
        tgs:ScenarioFacet.CivicIdeal, tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheStewardshipDeal, tgs:ScenarioFacet.ACapturedDemocracy, tgs:ScenarioFacet.ForcedFeudalism,
        tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheGlobalizationIdeal, tgs:ScenarioFacet.FriendlyNeighborsWithGoodFences, tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheGeopoliticalDangerZone, tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheColdWarScenario,
        tgs:ScenarioFacet.StrainedButWorkable, tgs:ScenarioFacet.QuietUnraveling, tgs:ScenarioFacet.HothouseTriage, tgs:ScenarioFacet.CascadingBreakdown ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Series.BendNotBreak, tgs:Interview.TGS_12_DennisMeadows, tgs:Work.ScenarioThinkingHistoricalEvolution ;
    skos:scopeNote "2ND PASS 2026-08-13 — MJSullivan flagged this as 'the key episode that describes these quadrants' given the axis-correction work done this session (see ScenarioDimension.Economic and ScenarioDimension.Geopolitical's own scopeNotes), and supplied the full transcript for a proper enrichment pass rather than relying on show notes alone. discusses list corrected from the original single tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification (now split into Concept + ScenarioFacet — see that split's own history) to the specific tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheGreatSimplification, and substantially expanded: this episode is the real primary source for all 16 ScenarioFacets across all 4 Dimensions, not just the original 4 Economic ones it had been scoped to before this pass. tgs:Concept.Superorganism added as a real, direct reference — Hagens' own words describing the Cold War Facet as 'the fission of the Superorganism.' Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: 'Fire apes' (a real recurring Reality Blind term, cited directly to a specific page); the real statistic that 26 people hold the same financial claims on biophysical reality as the bottom 4 billion humans (sourced to a real Oxfam report); Concept.DarkTriad already built elsewhere in this graph is referenced again here but not formally dct:referenced in this pass, to avoid overloading a single episode's reference list without a clear stopping point. hasCanonicalSource fixed 2026-08-13 (original 1st-pass note, still accurate) — same xsd:anyURI-literal-instead-of-proper-resource bug originally found and fixed on Frankly_138, never applied to the rest of this series until now."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely foundational episode for this graph's own scenario-matrix structure, not just another Frankly. Nate opens by directly naming a cognitive trap — settling on one preferred future and then judging everything else against it — and argues for holding a real distribution of possible futures instead. The four-grid structure is built up methodically and explicitly incompletely: Nate states outright that technology and demographics were both considered for their own separate grids and deliberately excluded, arguing both function as amplifiers of whatever direction the underlying system is already moving rather than independent axes — AI specifically is framed as already inside the Earth Systems grid, since data-center electricity demand is itself a direct new draw on the energy/biophysical system the whole framework is built around. A real, distinctive moment of self-aware epistemic humility: Nate explicitly invites the audience to propose grids he hasn't considered, naming mental/physical health and freedom of information/communication as real candidates he chose not to include. The Geopolitical grid's own real-time grounding is notable — recorded during the actual 2026 Iran war and Strait of Hormuz closure, Nate uses that live, unfolding event as a direct real-world example of the 'adversarial and interdependent' quadrant, citing the Philippines' energy emergency, Bangladesh's school closures, and Japan's emergency oil-reserve release as concurrent real evidence. Nate draws a real, pointed distinction between what a contraction-vs-growth headline means for the 'fairness' question (during growth) versus the 'is there enough' question (during contraction) — the same quadrant axes, he argues, describe a fundamentally different lived experience depending on which economic direction they're paired with. The episode closes by explicitly refusing to let any single quadrant stand as a full future on its own — Nate states directly that nobody will 'live in The Great Simplification in general,' only in a Great Simplification combined with a specific power structure, geopolitical backdrop, and ecological condition, previewing Part 3's work building concrete scenario composites from these four layers."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_140_APerspectiveFromLebanon a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Perspective From Lebanon: Who Will We Be When Things Get Hard?"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a complete real transcript and show notes. An unusually personal Frankly, prompted by a real conversation with a close friend of Nate's — his own meditation coach — who lives in Beirut and has been living through daily bombing, hosting displaced families in her small apartment, and grieving friends killed in the conflict. She is unnamed throughout, both in the original episode and here, and has explicitly declined to appear on the show herself; her privacy is respected in this build accordingly — no attempt is made to identify her. Nate uses her account, and her own real, striking equanimity despite the danger she's in, to distill the show's broader work down to three real, deliberately simpler and more personal questions: who are we going to be once comfort and convenience thin out; how are we going to actually live under a coming biophysical constraint; and what are we willing to expend our own life's energy to protect. Real, direct correction to the raw transcript's own phonetic error: 'Bio Ocumalafe' is Bayo Akomolafe, confirmed directly via this episode's own show notes ('A Slower Urgency')."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-04-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-05-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/140-a-perspective-from-lebanon> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pILjdgXeh_o> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Frankly-140-Transcript-A-Perspective-From-Lebanon.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_17_MovingFromApathyToAction, tgs:Interview.TGS_146_BillPlotkin, tgs:Interview.TGS_138_FritjofCapra, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_88_SnowSingularityRocksInTheRiver, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency, tgs:Persona.BayoAkomolafe, tgs:Work.PewAmericansMorallyBad2026 ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opens with a real, direct disclosure of exhaustion — four years of twice-weekly content, described in his own words as 'shedding my skin, perhaps shedding multiple skins' — before explaining the real conversation that prompted this pause: his friend's account of constant, un-actionable fight-or-flight (nowhere to flee to, no one to fight), and her own real, striking equanimity despite it, grounded in a weekly community meditation practice (now around 60 people, notably including both Muslim and Jewish participants) and a real, centuries-old lineage of mutual trust in her home village. A real, specific human cost is named directly: journalist Amal Khalil, known for helping injured animals amid the bombing, among a real, cited total of 260 journalists killed across Gaza, Lebanon, Yemen, and Iran. Nate is candid about the real discomfort of his own position — 'a woman in Beirut is coaching a guy in Minnesota' whose own country is not indirectly connected to the violence she's living through — without resolving or dismissing that tension. The episode's real emotional turn comes as Nate applies his friend's grounded state as a direct contrast to his own: sitting safely in a comfortable office, watching the news, he recognizes his own nervous system generating a real fight-or-flight response to no actual physical danger, while she — facing genuine danger — remains calmer. Closes without prescriptions, deliberately: 'I don't have any pithy ending or recommendations,' offering instead small, concrete grounding practices (going outside, calling someone loved, noticing a meadowlark identified on the Merlin app) as how nervous systems 'remember it's safe enough to think and act clearly again.' Ends on a real, candid admission of his own next direction: after '20 years' of what he calls 'biophysical macro,' his real, stated next curiosity is 'biophysical micro' — who people are and might become individually — explicitly naming himself 'a newbie' on this front."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Friend's identity deliberately not researched or asserted, consistent with her own real, explicit refusal to appear on the show — respecting a stated privacy preference for a private individual in an active, dangerous situation, not a gap in available information. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Frankly #106 ('10 Things Worth More Than a Pound of Gold'), directly cross-referenced — now built, see that episode's own entry. Bayo Akomolafe (real, directly cited philosopher/author) is now a real, built Persona in this graph, added 2026-08-30 alongside Frankly_106's own build — dct:references updated accordingly above. Real, direct confirmation, tracked down 2026-08-30: this episode's own show notes cite a genuinely distinct, real Pew piece — 'In 25-Country Survey, Americans Especially Likely To View Fellow Citizens as Morally Bad' (Mar 2026, now minted as tgs:Work.PewAmericansMorallyBad2026) — not the same as the May 2025 general-trust piece already tracked in the WWNS candidate list (docs/wwns-topic-coverage-tracker.md, tgs-digest). This one is genuinely sharper: the US is the only country of 25 surveyed where more adults rate compatriots' morality as bad than good, driven by a real, direct partisan mechanism rather than general moral strictness — worth considering as a real, stronger replacement or complement to the existing candidate #12 entry."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_141_WideBoundaryNews a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wide Boundary News: Sacrificing Wilderness, Oil Data Propaganda, and Feeding the Superorganism's Brain"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly works through six real, separate current news items — US oil production data, the Iran/Strait of Hormuz crisis, fertilizer and cooking-fuel cascades, Minnesota's Boundary Waters mining decision, AI data center expansion, and a record-setting dairy cow — widening each one several orders of magnitude to surface a real, single underlying pattern: nearly every institution and narrative currently in use was calibrated for the carbon pulse's upslope, with no real plan for its downslope or plateau."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-05-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-05-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/141-wide-boundary-news-5-8-26> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g-BJ9XkP2Pc> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Frankly-141-Transcript-WBN-5_8_26.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Human.ArtBerman ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly dissects a real, current US Department of Energy claim that US oil production exceeds Saudi Arabia and Russia combined, showing this is technically accurate only because the US figure bundles crude oil with natural gas liquids, biofuels, and refinery gain, while the Saudi and Russian figures are reported on a real, narrower crude-only basis — a direct, concrete demonstration of how a headline claim can be entirely true and still structurally misleading through category selection alone. He's real and direct that the US remains a net importer of actual crude oil despite widely-repeated claims of being a net oil exporter, since the real export surplus is almost entirely natural gas liquids used for things like plastic packaging, not gasoline or diesel. A real, direct cascading-consequences chain traces the Strait of Hormuz closure from oil price spikes down through fertilizer shortages (roughly 3 million tons monthly of blocked urea, ammonia, and phosphate) to a real, human-scale image: households in Mumbai lining up at 3am for LPG cooking-gas cylinders as families in Kenya revert from clean gas back to charcoal after a decade of progress. He directly uses a real historical parallel, post-Soviet Russia's own environmental collapse once central regulation weakened in the 1990s, to challenge the naive assumption that a shrinking economy automatically produces less pollution — resource extraction continued regardless, just without oversight. A real, direct physiological analogy anchors his AI/data-center concern: human brains consume roughly 20% of the body's metabolic energy despite being only 2% of its mass, offered as a direct parallel for the Superorganism now diverting outsized energy toward its own 'cognitive layer,' with Nate directly, honestly admitting nobody yet knows whether that allocation is justified. Closes on a deliberately small, real, concrete story most viewers wouldn't have otherwise seen: a real, record-setting dairy cow producing ten times her 1925 counterpart's milk yield, offered directly as 'the carbon pulse made into flesh' and a synecdoche for everything selectively bred or engineered for an energy regime now ending."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct methodological note: this episode was studied specifically for technique to fold into the WWNS series' own methodology — see the separate write-up accompanying this build for the concrete insights extracted. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise extraordinarily dense with real, current institutional and news reporting (DOE materials, EIA data, Holstein Association records) rather than pointing to further individuals — Art Berman is already built and directly cross-referenced, not a new find."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_142_GuideToStayingHumanPart2 a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Guide to Staying Human (Part 2): Navigating Dread and Carrying the Weight of Tomorrow"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-24 from a stub, using the full real transcript and official show notes. Opens on Art Berman's own framing of Operation Epic Fury as 'the biggest blunder in human history' (TGS_220), then turns inward: Nate names what he's actually feeling as dread, and draws on climate psychiatrist Lise Van Susteren's term 'pre-traumatic stress' (the body responding to a trauma that hasn't happened yet). Walks through the neuroscience in real depth — amygdala/HPA-axis/cortisol machinery evolved for immediate physical threats, chronically activated but never discharged by slow-moving abstract risk, producing allostatic load that measurably impairs the hippocampus and prefrontal cortex. Cites a real study finding ~28% of subjects are 'extreme dreaders' who'd rather take a stronger shock immediately than wait for a milder one — direct evidence dread itself can cost more than the feared event. Explicitly connects back to Part 1: 'agency is the opposite stance' to dread, since a meaningful action gives the threat system 'a different signal.' Closes with five practical pathways (mental reframing, somatic/breath practice, agency, community/co-regulation, and what Nate calls 'befriending the darkness') and five concrete steps for when dread arises, ending on a direct forward-pointer to Part 3's topic: the movement from dread into presence, and the default mode network."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.AGuideToStayingHuman ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 2 ;
    dct:created "2026-05-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-05-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/142-a-guide-to-staying-human-part-2> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Frankly-142-Transcript-A-Guide-to-Staying-Human-Part-2.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4gJ9E233hfw>, <https://open.spotify.com/episode/4lvZfG1sdQW7vnGZs8zc01> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-a-guide-to-staying-human-part-9e6> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency, tgs:Concept.Dread ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_220_AWorldOnThePrecipice, tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_17_MovingFromApathyToAction ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: TGS #202 (Samantha Sweetwater), cited alongside RR-17 as a Joanna Macy/Work That Reconnects cross-reference, not yet an Episode individual. Real Persona candidates cited by name but deliberately NOT bootstrapped this pass, matching this graph's established deferred-citation pattern (Audrey Tang, Helena Norberg-Hodge, etc. in Frankly_132's own scopeNote): Viktor Frankl, Peter Levine (somatic experiencing), Daniel Siegel ('name it to tame it'), Joanna Macy (noted in the transcript as recently deceased), and Stephen Levine — none built as Persona individuals here. Lise Van Susteren (climate psychiatrist, 'pre-traumatic stress' coinage, real personal detail — a childhood neighbor of Nate's), now built (2026-09-02). Francis Weller, cited alongside Macy, already exists in this graph as tgs:Persona.FrancisWeller — not re-flagged."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_143_GuideToStayingHumanPart3 a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Guide to Staying Human (Part 3): The Default Mode Network and the Metacrisis"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT 2026-08-24 from a complete real transcript and official show notes — no prior stub existed. Opens on a real personal anecdote (missing a dawn birdsong and a full cup of coffee while mentally simulating 2026 diesel-rationing scenarios) to introduce the default mode network (DMN) — the brain system active during self-referential processing, theory of mind, mind-wandering, and mental time travel, anti-correlated with the task-positive network (TPN). Cites a real Harvard study ('A Wandering Mind is an Unhappy Mind') and the real canonical DMN discovery paper, and explicitly points back to this graph's own tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie as the only prior TGS coverage of the DMN. Names 5 specific real reasons metacrisis-aware people are especially vulnerable to chronic DMN dominance (emotional loading, no resolution signal, social isolation, vocational necessity of simulation, and a moral license that frames presence as a kind of abandonment), and explicitly links this episode back to Part 2: 'the same phenomenon I called full dread in last week's Frankly, but viewed from a slightly different angle' — coining a new term, 'future capture,' for the DMN-dominance experience when the content is civilizational. Closes with five pathways back to presence (the senses, the pause, single-tasking, beauty, and the finitude of things) and three concrete daily practices."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.AGuideToStayingHuman ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 3 ;
    dct:created "2026-05-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-05-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/143-a-guide-to-staying-human-part-3> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Frankly-143-Transcript-A-Guide-to-Staying-Human-Part-3.docx-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fZB6Ep8qSos>, <https://open.spotify.com/episode/3DzB8zatmpgek0Lfzkz67M> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-a-guide-to-staying-human-part-e54> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.Dread, tgs:Concept.Presence, tgs:Concept.DefaultModeNetwork, tgs:Concept.FutureCapture ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie, tgs:Interview.TGS_85_WisdomNatureAndTheBrain, tgs:Interview.TGS_165_WisdomOverPower, tgs:Persona.IainMcGilchrist ;
    skos:scopeNote """'Supernormal' appears once in this episode's own transcript ('our supernormal, unbelievable modern era') but as a casual adjective, not an invocation of tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli as a term of art — that Concept isn't yet built in this graph, and this single non-technical usage is deliberately not minting it; the show notes' own link to it is show-notes editorializing, not something Nate himself cites here.
        Task Positive Network (TPN) is real and load-bearing to this episode's own DMN/TPN anti-correlation framing but kept out as a separate Concept this pass — folded into Concept.DefaultModeNetwork's own definition instead, matching this graph's 'don't over-split a paired mechanism' discipline. Real Persona candidates cited but not bootstrapped: Thich Nhat Hanh.
        Iain McGilchrist and his 2 prior TGS appearances (#85, #165) already existed in this graph — linked, not rebuilt."""@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_144_AWordICantSeemToUnderstand a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Word I Can't Seem to Understand: Non-Duality and Our Living World"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, unusually honest personal admission: despite asking at least seven prior podcast guests to define non-duality (see tgs:Concept.NonDuality), most recently Andrew Holecek the same week, Nate says he still cannot define it. Real, direct central insight, arrived at on a bike ride: the difficulty may not be personal inadequacy but the particular Western cultural operating system itself -- reinforced by grammar, institutions, and the very act of definition-by-boundary -- which makes a concept with no outside structurally impossible to fence in. Closes not with a definition but with the possibility that loosening one's grip on certainty is itself the actual, available work."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-05-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-05-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/144-a-word-i-cant-seem-to-understand> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Frankly-144-Transcript-Nonduality.docx-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.NonDuality, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_221_AndrewHolecek, tgs:Interview.TGS_217_WisdomInAWorldInCrisis,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_216_SamHarris, tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie, tgs:Interview.TGS_88_RobertSapolsky,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_143_GuideToStayingHumanPart3 ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening admission: after at least seven real, direct attempts across podcast guests to get a working definition of non-duality, Nate names a real, different explanation that occurred to him mid-bike-ride -- not personal inadequacy (insufficient intelligence, ADHD, insufficient spiritual advancement, all named directly as prior self-explanations he's now questioning), but a real, structural feature of the culture he was raised in. Real, direct grammar argument: a simple sentence like 'I see the tree' encodes a watcher, an act of watching, and a thing watched -- Western grammar itself is called 'a little machine that generates separation,' making it structurally the wrong tool to point at something with 'no cracks or seams... for us to scrutinize.' Real, direct reframe of the whole exercise: asking for a definition was itself the wrong move from the start, since defining something means drawing a boundary around it, and the thing wisdom traditions point at 'has no outside' -- directly invoking the real, classic finger-pointing-at-the-moon teaching image to explain why traditional teachers point rather than define, and why Nate kept mistaking the finger for the moon across seven real conversations. Real, direct list of moments where the separation briefly softens, explicitly not offered as proof or definition: being lost in music (the default mode network 'just goes quiet'), the half-second before a beautiful thing gets labeled by the mind, grief following a real, recent pet loss that 'hollows you out' past the sense of a self running the show, and standing in a forest at dusk where it becomes genuinely hard to say where one stops and the ecosystem begins. Real, direct connection to the metacrisis, delivered as the episode's own real payoff: the same felt separateness that makes non-duality hard to grasp intellectually is the same root assumption that lets humanity treat nature as 'a backdrop, a resource, a pile of stuff' rather than the living system a person's own body is materially made from -- 'we've built an entire civilization on the assumption that the world is a warehouse and we're the customers... there is no safe distance, there never was.' Real, direct honest closing admission: the drive to nail down a clean, filed-away definition may itself be 'the most Western thing' about the difficulty -- the grasping itself is the wall -- and the real, available gift is not final understanding but a loosened grip on certainty, 'a hairline crack' letting a bit more light through than the day before."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct honest note preserved from the source yaml: this episode's own '_resolution' section rates its own URL match as 'weak' confidence, since the site's real slug doesn't closely resemble the uploaded filename -- the show's own team's own hedge, not something independently discovered here. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: David Abram's 'The Spell of the Sensuous' (cited directly for the language-and-grammar argument) has no existing Work entry in this graph."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_145_Part3UphillFutures a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "How to Think About the Future (Part 3): Uphill Futures in a Downhill World"@en ;
    skos:definition "Introduces a landscape metaphor (borrowed from developmental biology's Waddington landscape and lake-ecology succession) for thinking about civilizational futures: valleys (stable states systems settle into), ridges (the barriers/cost of transitioning between them), switchbacks (deliberate human effort — trust-building, institution-building — that makes an otherwise-too-steep transition possible), and erosion (neglect or active harm that makes a transition permanently harder). Central claim: the easiest-to-reach futures are not the same as the most desirable ones — desirable futures generally require sustained uphill effort to both reach and remain in, while undesirable ones tend to be the default downhill path requiring no coordinated effort at all."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.HowToThinkAboutTheFuture ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 3 ;
    dct:created "2026-05-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-06-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/145-how-to-think-about-the-future-part-3> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Frankly-145-Transcript-HTTATF-Pt-3.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/vTBQ1AS-KVw> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-uphill-futures-in-a-downhill> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.TheSenecaEffect, tgs:Concept.LandscapeOfPossibleFutures, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.ShortfallRisk ;
    skos:scopeNote "2026-08-13: real dates/subject/replay/essay added, transcript URL corrected (real URL uses a different month folder AND retains a .docx.pdf double-extension — the pattern-matched guess used across this whole series turned out inconsistent with the real site for parts 3/5/6, confirmed only via direct page fetches, not assumed)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_146_UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimesPart4 a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Uncomfortable Questions for Unsettled Times, Part 4: The Ultimate Alternative -- Are You Okay With Nuclear Warfare?"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, impromptu Part 4 in Nate's real 'Uncomfortable Questions for Unsettled Times' series (Part 3 confirmed as Frankly-134), recorded the same real week a US-Iran ceasefire memorandum was announced and President Trump's own real Truth Social post referenced 'the ultimate alternative' regarding nuclear weapons. Real, direct format: 13 real, deliberately unanswered questions about nuclear weapons, deterrence, and the erosion of the 80-year nuclear taboo -- explicitly not offered as analysis with conclusions, but as Nate's own real, stated form of preparedness and community-building in unsettled times."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-06-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-06-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimes ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 4 ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/146-uncomfortable-questions-for-unsettled-times-part-4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Frankly-146-Transcript-Uncomfortable-Questions-for-Unstable-Times-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_134_AWorldAtTheEdgeOfChange, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_144_AWordICantSeemToUnderstand,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_11_InSpiteOf, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_61_TheStraitOfHormuzAndTheSpice ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening: Nate names this as an impromptu, unwanted episode -- 'I do not wanna do this, frankly' -- prompted directly by a US-Iran 60-day ceasefire memorandum and President Trump's own real Truth Social post referencing radioactive 'nuclear dust' and 'the ultimate alternative, hopefully never to be used again.' Real, direct format stated up front: 13 real questions, deliberately not offered with answers, framed as a form of preparedness and, when discussed with others, a form of community. Real, direct callback to Part 3 (Frankly-134, recorded April 2nd): revisits that episode's own real question about tactical nuclear use against Iran's underground sites, noting two and a half months on, 'that question has gotten more uncomfortable, not less,' since most off-ramps available in April had since closed. Real, direct core argument: nuclear coercion, if the current deal is read as a foreign-policy win, has just been demonstrated to work against a non-nuclear state -- 'the opposite of what eighty years of non-proliferation was supposed to teach.' Real, direct historical evidence cited directly: Libya gave up its nuclear program in 2003 and Gaddafi was overthrown eight years later; North Korea kept its program and remains 'largely undisturbed' -- Nate's own real, direct, uncomfortable question: what does the world look like once a dozen states draw the practical lesson that keeping nuclear weapons gets you respected. Real, direct 'spite' framing, explicitly revisiting and updating Frankly-11's own earlier real concept: nuclear weapons possibly functioning as an instrument of resource spite between superpowers, given the Persian Gulf and Iran's South Pars gas field represent real, direct strategic energy assets independent of the nuclear narrative. Real, direct game-theoretic argument: a single tactical nuclear use, if the target doesn't immediately capitulate, forces an escalate-or-be-exposed choice where every incentive favors escalation -- directly questioning whether 'one and done' is a coherent strategic concept or a phrase that only makes the first use psychologically possible. Real, direct generational argument: the 80-year taboo may rest less on treaties than on 'lived terror' (duck-and-cover drills, The Day After, Hibakusha survivors touring schools) that was never transmitted to the generation now holding power -- directly asking what that means for the people with their fingers on the buttons today. Real, direct, honest closing self-doubt: Nate directly asks himself whether raising these questions publicly, for an already anxious and polarized audience, is net helpful or net harmful, without resolving the question. Real, direct final, most uncomfortable question of all, explicitly named as such: not whether Iran has nuclear weapons, but whether the entire episode has demonstrated that 'the USA is not as strong a superpower as it believes.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct series confirmation: this episode's own real show notes directly link 'Uncomfortable Questions for Unsettled Times #3' to Frankly-134 ('A World at the Edge of Change') -- resolving the open question this stub's own prior scopeNote had flagged (unconfirmed Parts 1-3). Frankly-134 itself remains a real, minimal stub in this graph; this same real, direct series link should be added there too as a forward-reference once it gets its own full build. Not minted as a formal thinkr:Series individual in this pass, since only Parts 3 and 4 are confirmed so far -- worth doing once Parts 1-2 (if they exist) are also confirmed. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Seymour Hersh (the journalist directly credited for reporting the nuclear-option story) has no existing entry in this graph."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_147_HowToPlay5dChess a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "How to Play 5D Chess: It's Not What You Think"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct chess-board metaphor (see tgs:Concept.FiveLensChess) for five successively widening vantage points from which the same real situation can be viewed -- the instinctive friend-or-threat read, tribal identification, a dispassionate aerial view of structural forces, the living world the whole board rests on, and a second, different game board visible in the distance. Real, direct central claim: the first two lenses are evolution's fastest, most-reinforced 'factory settings' and never disappear, but the actual skill needed now is learning to hold all five at once -- and holding only the wide lenses without real agency just produces despair at higher resolution."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-06-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-06-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/147-how-to-play-5d-chess> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Frankly-147-Transcript-5D-Chess.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.FiveLensChess, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_65_AndThenWhat, tgs:Interview.TGS_112_SamanthaSweetwater,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_158_JohnVervaeke, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_142_GuideToStayingHumanPart2,
        tgs:Persona.NoraBateson, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency, tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening pattern named: a headline, a clip, or a half-recognized guest name triggers a real, felt verdict before two sentences are even heard -- Nate directly includes himself in this, noting it happens 'more recently than I used to.' Real, direct lens-by-lens structure: lens one, the piece directly across, where the nervous system asks only 'friend or threat,' evolution's oldest, fastest software, resolved via proxies (do they look, sound, or share values like me) often before a full sentence lands -- illustrated with renewable energy, AI, and the Israel/Gaza conflict as real, direct examples of a topic itself tripping the wire independent of who's speaking. Real, direct forward-reference to a real, scheduled future guest, Lisa Feldman Barrett (September), cited for her real theory that the brain predicts and judges before conscious reasoning catches up. Lens two: the piece's whole team comes into view, and the real question shifts from 'do I trust this person' to 'is this person one of us' -- once a piece reads as the other team, its words stop landing as information and start reading as a game move. Lens three, a real, direct aerial view: sides are visible but not as good/bad, only as structural forces (energy, debt, geopolitics) moving pieces -- Nate's own real, direct, honest admission that describing the current power structure dispassionately from this height is routinely misheard, from lens one or two, as tacit endorsement of it; a direct nod to Nora Bateson's own real perspective is raised here. Lens four: the whole board rests on something living -- climate, biodiversity, 7 of 9 planetary boundaries already breached, roughly ten million species -- named directly as 'home base' for much of the show's own audience. Lens five, the widest: the board and the living world sit in a landscape, with a second, different, simpler game board visible in the distance, what Nate calls 'the long repair,' and the real question shifts from 'who's the villain here' to 'how do we get from this board to that one without flipping the table over.' Real, direct closing synthesis: lenses one and two are real, permanent factory settings that never go away and shouldn't be dropped, but the actual skill of this moment is learning to hold all five at once, then coming back down to actually act -- with a real, direct, honest caveat that holding only the wide lenses without any attached agency just produces despair at a higher resolution. Real, direct personal closing admission: the hardest part of hosting a multi-issue platform isn't the analysis itself, which Nate says he loves, but showing a large, diverse audience the whole board without any one person feeling like a piece being pushed across it -- described directly as 'quite Sisyphean most of the time.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct forward-reference, not yet buildable: Lisa Feldman Barrett is named as a real, scheduled future guest (September), not yet a real Persona in this graph since she hasn't appeared on the show as of this episode -- a genuine, direct candidate for a future TGS episode build once that conversation airs, not a citation to resolve now. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Susanne Cook-Greuter's real ego-development theory (cited directly for a related, complementary account of seeing from wider lenses) has no existing entry in this graph."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_148_Part4CompositeWorlds a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "How to Think About the Future (Part 4): Mordor to the Long Repair - How Might Daily Life Feel in the Next Decades?"@en ;
    skos:definition "The framework-defining episode of this series: proposes 4 Dimensions (grids) - Economic, Power & Distribution, Geopolitical, Earth Systems - each with 4 Facet options, for 256 mathematically possible composite futures, and builds out 4 representative named examples in detail (The Long Repair, Mordor Persists, Fortress World, The Unraveling - modeled as thinkr:Scenario individuals in scenarios.ttl). Explicit that these 4 are chosen to stretch thinking, not presented as an exhaustive or equally-likely set, and that the real future will likely be some regional patchwork of all of them rather than any single one holding globally."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.HowToThinkAboutTheFuture ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 4 ;
    dct:created "2026-06-09"^^xsd:date ;
