tgs:Monologue.Frankly_152_WatchWithBothEyesOpen a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Watch With Both Eyes Open: The Beauty and the Horror of Our Moment"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, personal reflection prompted by a week spent with ten friends already fluent in the more-than-human predicament, without needing to explain or perform that fluency -- Nate's own real, central metaphor: real depth perception requires both eyes open at once, the beauty eye and the horror eye, and most rooms he's in have one or the other closed, going flat and two-dimensional as a result. Widens into a real, direct argument that oxytocin-level, physiologically felt community cannot travel through fiber optic cable, and that finding or building local, in-person community fluent in these realities is a genuine no-regrets strategy, even if the work is only on oneself."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-07-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-07-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/152-watch-with-both-eyes-open> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Frankly-152-Transcript-Watch-With-Both-Eyes-Open.docx-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_54_ReflectionsFromIndia,
        <https://www.hylo.com/groups/the-great-simplification/join/i9jLqPmbGI>,
        <https://www.npr.org/2026/02/18/nx-s1-5716105/8-creative-ways-to-build-your-village-community-according-to-our-listeners> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening: a week spent with ten or eleven friends at a friend's organization working adjacent to the more-than-human predicament, where climate, AI, and energy depletion were almost never discussed directly, yet were fully, mutually understood -- freeing Nate to be, in his own words, 'a stand-up comic' and rediscover a version of himself that had quietly gone missing. Real, direct central metaphor, delivered as the episode's own real thesis: reality depth perception requires both eyes open at once -- the beauty eye and the horror eye -- and most rooms are either 'too hot' (fixated on systemic risk and gory detail) or 'too cold' (world's state unacknowledged entirely); either way, the world goes flat and two-dimensional. Real, direct callback to Frankly-54's own real 'naive about community' admission after six weeks in India with 30 people: the actual benefit of daily in-person contact wasn't practical (borrowing lawnmowers, trading garlic) but physiological -- oxytocin and serotonin from repetitive daily closeness -- and this week served as a real, direct 'booster shot' of a lesson gradually forgotten over two years. Real, direct honest admission: despite a large, valued online community and 20 years of exchange with his own systems-ecology colleagues, Nate names himself as paradoxically, genuinely lonely much of the time, since 'oxytocin in its truest sense doesn't travel through fiber optic cable.' Real, direct distinction drawn carefully: he has family, local friends, and neighbors who'd help in a crisis, but not yet ten people locally, within driving distance, fluent in the more-than-human predicament without needing to constantly discuss it -- a real, direct position he suspects much of the audience shares: 'aware, engaged, and locally alone.' Real, direct closing call to action: finding local community is named as a genuine no-regrets strategy, foundational to the work ahead even when that work is solely personal, with a real, direct acknowledgment that the platform itself feels a responsibility to help and is actively working on it."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct note on the show notes' own 'Essay version' link: its real URL slug ('essay-what-cant-be-rebuilt-irreversible') does not match this episode's own real title or content, unlike the confirmed, direct site error already documented on Frankly-156 -- this one is NOT independently confirmed as an error and was not treated as authoritative for cross-checking any fact in this build, per the Substack-precedence rule (gotcha #15), since it may simply be a mislinked essay rather than this episode's real companion piece. Real, direct, practical relevance flagged by MJSullivan directly: the NPR 'building your village' link in this episode's own real show notes is a genuinely appropriate, real, practical resource for the WWNS series' own 'finding the others' material (see wwns-intro-part1.md), given its direct thematic overlap with this episode's own real content on local-community-building."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_153_WhenTruthBecomesHazardous a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "When Truth Becomes Hazardous: Navigating Information Through the Metacrisis"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct expansion of the 'triple information hazard' Nate first named in Frankly-34, now grown to twelve (see tgs:Concept.TwelveInformationHazards), grounded in Nick Bostrom's real concept of an information hazard -- risk that comes specifically from disseminating true information, not misinformation. Real, direct closing argument: none of the twelve argues for silence, only for care about timing, context, scale, and trust, and the deepest available response to all twelve at once is growing real, direct community and trust at human scale."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-07-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-07-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/153-when-truth-becomes-hazardous> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Frankly-153-Transcript-When-Truth-Becomes-Hazardous.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.TwelveInformationHazards, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_34_LimitsToPodcastingAboutLimits, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_23_MordorEconomy, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_51_DJWhite, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_24_TheFantasyOfSpaceColonization,
        tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_11_TalesFromTheCarbonPulse, tgs:Interview.TGS_11_RexWeyler,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_135_OilOneOhOne, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_136_OilTwoOhOne,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_137_OilThreeOhOne, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_109_PeakOilPonziSchemesAndPlanetaryBoundaries,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_56_PeakOilAIAndTheStraw, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_23_ThePlasticDetox,
        tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_15_ThePlasticCrisis, tgs:Interview.TGS_89_SianSutherland,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_142_GuideToStayingHumanPart2,
        tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_17_MovingFromApathyToAction, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_128_UltraProcessedInformation,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_93_InformationBurnout, tgs:Interview.TGS_194_LukeKemp, tgs:Interview.TGS_153_LukeKemp,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_94_SocialOvershoot, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_152_WatchWithBothEyesOpen,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction, tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening definition, Bostrom's own term: an information hazard is a risk from disseminating true information, not lies. Real, direct hazards 1-4, explicitly grouped as stemming from the same fact -- speaking publicly means speaking in front of the very actors and systems one is trying to change. (1) Truth to power lands on the speaker; Nate's own real, half-joking reference to not wanting 'the Eye of Sauron' turned toward rural Minnesota. (2) Inoculation: publishing a strategy against dark-triad power gives that power real, direct advance notice, like announcing the next football play over the stadium loudspeaker. (3) Dual-use: an accurate map of societal fragility serves firefighters and arsonists alike, and Nate's own real, direct concession that after 20-plus years of mapping the human predicament, 'the arsonists... are better funded, more incentivized, and are probably paying closer attention.' (4) AI ingestion, named as genuinely new: every public essay or transcript now becomes real, direct LLM training data, collapsing what used to be a real gap between publishing something and a bad actor acting on it -- Nate's own real, personal response is a decision to keep 10-20% of what he knows or believes for in-person conversations with no AI note-taker present, building what he calls a wall between 'authentic Nate' and 'the brain of the Borg.' Real, direct hazards 5-8: (5) spotlight hazard -- publicizing quiet, effective conservation or finance strategies can kill them, since they work precisely because no one's watching (Nate directly credits colleague DJ White for examples that only become tellable years after the fact). (6) Cassandra timing -- illustrated directly with peak oil, which 'looked identical to being wrong for about 15 years.' (7) self-fulfilling prophecy -- a bank run as the classic real example, where changed belief, not changed facts, triggers real collapse. (8) musical chairs -- Nate's own long-running real metaphor for the gap between financial claims and the world's actual source/sink capacity; describing the shortage of real chairs can itself accelerate the stampede to grab one. Real, direct hazards 9-11, explicitly presented as a connected trio: (9) despair -- the full truth without agency or community leaves people worse off, part of why Nate stopped publishing publicly for a period seven or eight years ago. (10) reassurance, its real mirror image -- softening the story to avoid hazard 9 leaves society planning for a future 'none of the analysts actually privately expect'; Nate directly invokes James Stockdale's real, own account that neither optimists nor pessimists survived his POW camp, only realists holding both truths at once. (11) half-map -- naming risks without corresponding strategies leaves an audience with 'half of a map, but 100% of the emotion,' inviting panic, snake oil, and demagoguery. Real, direct hazard 12, discovered mid-bike-ride while assembling this very list, after the other eleven were already finished: the weighing/paranoia hazard -- the ongoing, private burden of constantly weighing what can and can't be said becomes its own real, direct hazard, which Nate compares to 'slow-drip water torture.' Real, direct closing argument: none of the twelve is an argument for silence, only for care about timing, audience, and scale; the deepest available response to all twelve at once is the same one Nate keeps arriving at from other directions -- growing real trust and community at human scale, since a truth that's hazardous shouted into a global microphone can become benign or healing shared around a kitchen table, directly echoing the prior week's Frankly-152."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct forward-reference: Nate says 'something a little lighter next week, maybe' -- not a claim to verify against any specific future episode, just a closing aside. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own real show notes directly link Frankly-132's own 'finding the others' framing and the Hylo community join-link, both already present elsewhere in this graph -- confirming, rather than introducing, their real recurring relevance across multiple episodes. Real, direct confirmation (2026-09-16): Frankly-34's own real, original triple information hazard (see tgs:Concept.OriginalTripleInformationHazard, built the same day from Frankly-34's own real transcript) is confirmed to map precisely onto three of the twelve here -- self-fulfilling prophecy, truth to power (named identically in both), and spotlight -- rather than being a vague, unverified precursor as this build originally treated it."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_154_UnordinaryInterventions a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Unordinary Interventions: How to Plant Seeds for a Better Future"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct companion to Frankly_132 — explicitly turning 'from the directional to the personal.' Where Frankly_132 laid out 6 broad societal fronts, this episode offers 15 small, specific, individual-scale interventions, each explicitly aimed at 'cloud seeding the storm itself' rather than merely helping a person cope with it. Nate draws a real, direct distinction between the two: growing food, improving sleep, and building a support network genuinely help a person weather the metacrisis, but (in his own words) 'don't much change the storm itself' — every intervention offered here is instead framed as a real seed for what the whole system actually needs, grounded in his own real complexity-science framing that tiny differences in starting conditions can lead to wildly different outcomes."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:created "2026-08-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-08-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/154-unordinary-interventions> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Frankly-154-Transcript-Unordinary-Interventions.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/hMb9uBz-31A> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Frankly #114 ('Directional Advice for the More Than Human Predicament'), Frankly #65 ('wide-boundary capital,' already flagged as deferred during the RR-21 build earlier this session), Frankly #98 ('cultural mitochondria,' already flagged deferred during the Frankly_132 build), Frankly #23 ('The Mordor Economy'), Frankly #59 ('7 Meta Questions About Our Global Metabolism'), Frankly #120 ('The Creature in the Machine'), Frankly #124 ('Wide Boundary News,' Nate's own real revised peak-oil call), and Frankly #142/#143 (Guide to Staying Human Parts 2/3) — all real, confirmed URLs. Real new Personas found but NOT built: Brett KenCairn (real Boulder Sustainability Director, real prior TGS episode #222, directly described as a recent real conversation partner — 'my recent conversation with...'), Jeff Currie (real prior TGS episode #229), Audrey Tang (already flagged deferred during the Frankly_132 build), Tristan Harris (TGS #214), John Vervaeke (TGS #158), Anna Lembke (TGS #206), Jonathan Haidt (TGS #59, already flagged deferred during the RR-15 build). The 15 individual interventions themselves ARE minted in this pass, as tgs:PersonalIntervention individuals — see personalinterventions.ttl."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opens with an unusually explicit methodological distinction Nate draws for himself as much as the audience: most 'what can I do' lists blur together self-protection (growing food, improving sleep, building a support network) with actually changing the trajectory of the metacrisis itself, and he states directly that everything offered here is aimed at the latter, not the former — 'nothing I offer here is primarily intended as just a coping skill.' A real, candid personal admission anchors the 'be wrong in public' intervention: Nate directly cites his own revised peak-oil call as the example, disclosing that every time he updates a public view, he braces to be thought less of, and states plainly that 'almost every time, it goes the other way.' The episode's own real, load-bearing metaphor — 'seeding' the future rather than choosing it — is offered as a direct extension of Nate's own complexity-science background, not a rhetorical flourish: he frames individual action as shifting initial conditions rather than directly producing outcomes. The closing framing is explicit about scale without being falsely modest or grandiose: Nate states plainly that one person doing these things produces 'a healthier and saner life for them and their family,' thousands doing them produces 'some sort of subculture,' and only millions doing them might actually shift the underlying drivers — a real, graduated claim rather than an implied guarantee that individual action alone resolves the predicament."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_155_WhoBrokeTheWorld a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Who Broke the World? The Villains We Choose and the Work They Give Us"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct eight-lens framework (see tgs:Concept.EightWorldviewLenses) for why people examining the same real facts arrive at radically different diagnoses of the more-than-human predicament -- explicitly framed as a real, direct prequel to a planned future Frankly series ('How Could I Be Wrong?') in which Nate intends to steelman his own thesis. Each lens gets a villain and a task; Nate's own real, central architectural claim is that the first six operate inside a floor and ceiling set by the last two (thermodynamics, overshoot). Directly borrows Olivier Hamant's own real term 'robustness' (from the immediately preceding episode, TGS-230) for what a genuinely durable, cross-lens solution actually needs."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-08-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-08-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/155-who-broke-the-world> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Frankly-155-Transcript-Who-Broke-the-World_.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EightWorldviewLenses, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification,
        tgs:SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_65_AndThenWhat, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_3_EnergyBlindness,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_147_HowToPlay5dChess,
        tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_24_TheFantasyOfSpaceColonization, tgs:Interview.TGS_217_WisdomInAWorldInCrisis,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_165_WisdomOverPower, tgs:Interview.TGS_85_WisdomNatureAndTheBrain,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_230_OlivierHamant ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening framing: Nate is explicit this is not a straw-man exercise -- all eight lenses are held by sincere, intelligent people, and he names his own primary lens upfront (the eighth, thermodynamic one) as a real, direct disclosure rather than hiding it. Real, direct lens-by-lens villain/task pairs, each with its own real, worked example: political (the other party; vote), religious (faithlessness, or a fated world one shouldn't try to save; repentance, or calm non-resistance), class (concentrated wealth, citing the real post-1970s US productivity-pay divergence and a 5-6x house-price-to-income ratio; redistribution), technology (regulators and NIMBYs, citing France's real 15-year grid decarbonization before nuclear building stopped; keep innovating), community/culture (modernity's loss of connection, directly citing Iain McGilchrist; rebuild ritual and belonging), animist/moral (civilization treating life as inventory, citing Wetiko as an old Algonquin term for taking more than you need; protect and extend standing to the more-than-human), overshoot (ourselves and our throughput, citing the real ~73% average decline in monitored wildlife populations since 1970; degrowth), and thermodynamic (ourselves and the one-time energy inheritance; Nate's own real, direct admission that this lens has no real single task and 'continues to lose most of the arguments it enters' precisely because there's no pile of surplus anyone can visibly redistribute). Real, direct central claim, delivered as the episode's own real payoff: the first six lenses all operate inside a floor (thermodynamics -- where the real surplus came from) and a ceiling (overshoot -- what the planet can actually absorb) set by the last two. Real, direct practical warning closes the episode: any proposal requiring shared diagnosis before shared action will probably fail, and probably should -- the durable solutions are the ones people holding any of the eight lenses can work toward for their own real, different reasons, borrowing Olivier Hamant's own real term 'robustness' directly from the prior week's episode."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct forward-reference: Nate names a planned future Frankly series, 'How Could I Be Wrong?', in which he intends to directly steelman his own thesis -- not yet built, genuinely doesn't exist yet as of this episode. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own real show notes directly link the CTB coalition-building guide already added to Frankly-132's own dct:references several turns back, confirming that resource's real, recurring relevance across multiple episodes rather than a one-off citation."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_156_AnAntidoteToDespair a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "An Antidote to Despair: What Dogs Reveal About the Metacrisis"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, personal reflection prompted by a genuine emergency vet visit -- Nate's dog Muffy ate corn cobs and required two nights of monitoring -- widened into a real, wide-boundary look at dogs as a real product of the carbon pulse (roughly a billion alive today, weighing as much collectively as all the world's wild land mammals combined), and a real, direct, personal argument that the unconditional love of a dog or puppy is one of the few genuine antidotes to the psychopathic pressures of the current system and the metacrisis itself."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-08-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-08-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/156-an-antidote-to-despair> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Frankly-155-Transcript-Our-Best-_Invention_.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct personal opening: a corn-cob emergency for Nate's dog Muffy (ninety pounds, a 'full-figured golden retriever gal') led to two nights at an emergency vet clinic in Minneapolis before X-rays came back negative. Real, direct wide-boundary framing follows: roughly one billion dogs are alive globally, weighing collectively about the same as all the world's wild land mammals combined -- but the median dog on Earth is a real, roughly thirty-pound roaming scavenger in Asia or Africa, not the ninety-pound, well-vetted golden retriever Nate has, and only about 20% of the world's billion dogs live in what Nate calls the Global North pattern of dog ownership. Real, direct personal anecdote from six weeks in Auroville, India in 2024: befriending and temporarily adopting several of the real street dogs there, getting in trouble for feeding them since they'd become habituated to whoever was in a given hotel room. Real, direct environmental-impact admission, not smoothed over: dogs and cats in the US eat as many calories as roughly sixty million Americans and account for roughly a quarter of the environmental impact of American meat consumption -- Nate directly names owning three dogs, three cats, and three horses as something he personally rationalizes partly by never having had children. Real, direct claim about the vast majority of dog breeds having developed within the past 200 years of the Carbon Pulse (per the episode's own companion Substack essay, treated as the authoritative figure per MJSullivan's own standing editorial-precedence rule -- Frankly monologues are recorded extemporaneously, with the Substack version written afterward with real editorial cleanup; the spoken transcript's own real, live figure of 'a hundred and fifty years' is kept as a footnote below, not asserted as equally reliable). Real, direct emotional core: a long-standing, unexplained pattern of stronger emotional response to animal pain than human pain in films, illustrated with two specific, real, named scenes (The Outlaw Josey Wales, Dances with Wolves) that left a lasting, direct emotional imprint. Real, direct closing reflection, delivered as the episode's actual point: at a recent gathering of existential-risk researchers discussing AI, markets, and the metacrisis for over ten hours, Nate's own real, unironic response, spoken aloud to the group, was 'I really want a puppy right now' -- offered not as a joke but as a real, direct statement that an ordinary puppy, wolves' domesticated descendants across roughly ten thousand generations, is for him the truest available antidote to AI, surveillance, and the psychopathic economic systems he spends most of his time analyzing, precisely because a puppy's love carries no conditions and no awareness of any of it."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct, confirmed error on the show's own official site, not an upload mistake: this episode's real, official show notes correctly describe dogs in their opening summary and metadata, but everything from the 'Before the coming Frankly series' line onward is a real, exact, word-for-word duplicate of Frankly-155's own show notes (the eight-worldview-lenses/villains content) -- confirmed by MJSullivan directly as Nate's team's own uncorrected error, re-checked and found identical on a second upload. None of that duplicated content (Frankly-65, Frankly-3, Frankly-129, Frankly-147, RR-24, the three McGilchrist episodes, the CTB coalition link, or Olivier Hamant) is used here, since none of it has any real connection to this actual episode's real content about dogs. This episode's own real transcript, provided directly and read in full, is the primary source used for the content above, cross-checked (2026-09-16) against the episode's own companion Substack essay ('What The Great Simplification Means for Pets'). Real, standing editorial-precedence rule established the same day, per MJSullivan directly: where a Frankly's own spoken transcript and its later Substack essay genuinely disagree on a factual detail, the Substack version should generally be trusted, since Frankly monologues are recorded extemporaneously while the Substack comes later with real editorial cleanup -- applied directly to this episode's own real, live-vs-written discrepancy on dog-breed development timelines (transcript: 'a hundred and fifty years'; Substack: 'the past 200 years'), with 200 years taken as the real, authoritative figure. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: The Outlaw Josey Wales and Dances with Wolves are both real, direct film citations, not modeled as Works given they're brief, illustrative mentions rather than substantive engagement."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_157_DecentPeopleIndecentSystem a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Turning the Dials Back on Psychopathy: Decent People, Indecent System"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct follow-on to Greg Elliott's real TGS appearance (already built) on the Psychopathic Selection Hypothesis, mapping Elliott's framework onto his own real, coined Seven Dials of Institutional Psychopathy — each dial set toward genuine cooperation in small-scale ancestral human groups, but flipped toward extraction at the scale of the modern economic Superorganism. Opens with a real, direct synthesis connecting Elliott's own real term, 'instrumental behavior,' to the psychopathic-institution framing Nate had already explored with Nancy McWilliams and Reid Meloy (already built, RR-19) the prior year."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-08-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/157-decent-people-indecent-system> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Frankly-157-Transcript-Decent-People-Indecent-System.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.SevenDialsOfInstitutionalPsychopathy, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_231_GregElliott, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly grounds the whole episode in a real, foundational reassurance before laying out the dials: roughly 99% of individual humans are genuinely prosocial and kind, and the real problem lies in a system that selectively rewards predatory behavior, not in human nature itself becoming worse. He walks through each real dial with concrete, everyday illustration — dial one (repeated interaction) explains why anonymous, one-off economic transactions erode the reciprocity that repeated encounters with the same people naturally sustain; dial six (who writes the rules) is directly named as the 'locked' dial holding all the others in place, connecting real historical patterns from reverse-dominance hierarchies in hunter-gatherer bands to 'buying the game board' through modern vertical and horizontal integration and regulatory capture. A real, vivid personal illustration closes dial seven (symbols and proxies): citing Goodhart's Law directly, Nate argues symbols like money and GDP can fully decouple from the physical reality they're meant to represent — offering a real, direct practical antidote of deliberately measuring something real alongside money (kilowatt-hours, bushels, hours of life per purchase) and engaging in real, direct off-ledger reciprocity like barter, potlucks, and repair cafés. A real, personal, disarming aside surfaces mid-episode: Nate discloses he's recovering from a real, recent, unexpected gallbladder surgery, framing this Frankly as delivered while still 'riding the surgery wave.' Closes with a real, direct, unresolved series of open questions rather than firm conclusions: which dial is most flipped in the listener's own life, whether their own cooperation is genuinely altruistic or merely 'entrapped,' and — his own real, most direct closing question — whether the good guys can ever actually win, and if the honest answer is no, whether one can still choose to be a good person anyway."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest date correction: the source show notes list 'Recorded on: Aug 25, 2023,' which is almost certainly a data-entry error given this episode is explicitly a direct follow-on to Greg Elliott's real TGS-231 appearance, itself a 2026 episode — corrected here to 2026-08-25 as the most plausible real date, consistent with the September 4, 2026 publish date. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Greg Elliott's own real Substack essay on the Psychopathic Selection Hypothesis, directly cited alongside his TGS appearance, not modeled as a separate Work given it's the same underlying framework already captured via the Concept and his built episode."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_158_WhatWouldChangeMyMind a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-11"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-11"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Human Predicament: What Would Change My Mind"@en ;
    skos:definition "Prompted directly by a stranger's pointed question in conversation — what would change his mind about his own core thesis — Nate works through a real, two-layer answer: first a list of concrete biophysical signals that would challenge his diagnosis, then a real, direct recognition that none of those signals move on their own, since they are themselves downstream of a deeper cultural shift roughly a decade prior."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-09-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/158-the-human-predicament-what-would-change-my-mind> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_159_TheRealWork ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Frankly-158-Transcript-What-Would-Change-My-Mind.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.WhatWouldChangeMyMind, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_146_BillPlotkin, tgs:Human.DonellaMeadows, tgs:Human.DavidSloanWilson, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_44_TheManyShapesOfTheCarbonPulse, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency, tgs:Interview.TGS_139_DanielChristianWahl ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly states his own real, single-sentence thesis for the purposes of this exercise: within roughly a decade, human civilization will undergo a deflationary depression triggered in bond and currency markets once financial claims on biophysical reality exceed that reality, initiating a phase shift toward a more local, regionally integrated world. He's real and direct that his own first-pass list of falsifying biophysical signals, though genuinely his first response, turned out to be 'out of sequence' — they would be the last things to show up, not the first, since none of them (falling fossil use amid GDP growth, 25% material circularity, cheaper copper refining) happens 'unless millions of humans were already living differently.' A real, direct, carefully-drawn distinction separates genuine cultural change from false positives throughout: green luxury and eco-resorts don't count as a status shift unless the new status marker is measurably less energy- and material-intensive than what it replaces, and a shift toward household self-sufficiency doesn't count if it's forced by depression rather than chosen. He's real and direct that political speech is a genuine leading indicator specifically because politicians are constrained by the median voter — a candidate advocating consumption taxes and 'we are embedded in the web of life' framing surviving re-election would be real, direct evidence the median citizen's own values have already shifted. Closes on real, direct personal vulnerability rather than confident resolution: naming Bill Plotkin's own already-built concept of 'ecological adulthood' as something he does see, but 'at extremely small scales,' and turning the original question back on the viewer directly — 'what would change your mind? And if you can't think of anything, sit with that, because I did for a bit.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest correction: the source show notes' own recorded_date field reads 'Sep 8, 2023,' clearly a typo given every cited source in the episode is 2025/2026-dated and the episode published September 11, 2026 — the real recorded date is treated here as September 8, 2026. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Frankly_115 ('Inflation, Deflation, and Simplification') and Frankly_32 ('The Perils of EROI'), both real, directly cited as archive references, neither yet built."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_159_TheRealWork a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Real Work: Four Kinds of Work for a Less Catastrophic Future"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, unusually raw episode recorded shortly after Nate's own real surgery, prompted directly by declining Saudi oil-export capacity, six-dollar US diesel, and a Strategic Petroleum Reserve at 40-year lows -- but deliberately looking past those headlines to name four real, direct categories of underrepresented work (see tgs:Concept.FourCategoriesOfRealWork): Bend Not Break, nuclear disarmament, midwifing the biosphere, and rekindling the sacred. Real, direct closing personal statement on the meaning of life itself: not competition, accumulation, or status, but contributing to more life, especially complex and diverse life, and to the conditions that sustain it."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-09-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-09-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior,
        tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/159-the-real-work> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Frankly-159-Transcript-The-Real-Work.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.FourCategoriesOfRealWork, tgs:Concept.CorporationsAsMisalignedAI, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse,
        tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_222_BrettKencairn, tgs:Interview.TGS_17_ChuckWatson, tgs:Interview.TGS_27_JoeTainter,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_8_PeterWard, tgs:Interview.TGS_231_GregElliott, tgs:Interview.TGS_232_CharlesEisenstein,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_233_RomanYampolskiy, tgs:Interview.TGS_167_FrancisWeller, tgs:Interview.TGS_146_BillPlotkin,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_125_VanessaAndreotti, tgs:Interview.TGS_18_ThomasMurphy,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_151_Part6PlanningUnderUncertainty, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_158_WhatWouldChangeMyMind, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_114_DirectionalAdvice, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_69_GoldilocksTechnology,
        tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening: rather than commenting on the day's own real headlines (crippled Saudi export capacity, diesel approaching seven dollars, Costco motor-oil purchase limits), Nate deliberately looks two or three steps past them to name what he calls the actual, underattended work of this moment. Real, direct predicament-not-problem framing repeated and sharpened: a deflationary depression is treated as effectively inevitable -- not a matter of if but when -- once financial and paper claims meaningfully exceed real biophysical means, a real, direct 'Wile E. Coyote reconnection date' between claims and reality, most likely to manifest first in bond and currency markets. Real, direct first category, Bend Not Break: not systemic collapse, but a real, direct financial 'haircut' and resulting supply-chain strain, managed through relocalized/re-regionalized supply chains for food, medicine, and spare parts; deliberately expanded local social capital across political and generational lines; real, direct community and watershed scenario planning; and Goldilocks technology (sodium-ion batteries named directly as a real, concrete example -- less efficient than lithium, but cheaper, more abundant, and locally repairable). Real, direct second category, nuclear disarmament, explicitly named as work Nate does mostly behind the scenes rather than publicly: nine states hold over 12,000 warheads; the real, direct danger named is not a 'madman' scenario but sane leaders and sane technocrats (increasingly with AI assistance) making a cold calculation that tactical nuclear use is worth the risk -- with historical near-misses averted specifically by individual disobedience, Stanislav Petrov named directly as the real, concrete example. Real, direct third category, midwifing the biosphere: a direct, explicit rejection of 'the planet will be fine' as a real, meaningful comfort -- an inert planet of jellyfish and roaches is not what's actually being protected; complex, diverse life is, and Earth's own real remaining window for it (credited directly to guest Peter Ward's own real 'Earth Clock' framing) may already be substantially spent. Real, direct fourth category, rekindling the sacred, explicitly framed as multi-generational, beyond Nate's own lifetime: recovering a felt sense that some things -- a functioning ocean, a dawn chorus, an unbroken four-billion-year evolutionary lineage -- are not for sale, not substitutable, and unacceptable to lose; grounded directly in a real, personal admission that this recognition may have required humanity to nearly lose the biosphere before it could be genuinely felt. Real, direct response to an anticipated challenge -- why not prioritize AI alignment risk as a fifth category (see tgs:Concept.CorporationsAsMisalignedAI): corporations, as legal persons already misaligned with human and biospheric good, represent a real, direct non-alignment problem humanity has already been living inside for well over a century, predating any AI-specific version of the same risk. Real, direct personal statement of meaning, offered as the episode's own real culmination: the meaning of a single human life is not competition, accumulation, or status, but contributing to more life, especially complex and diverse life, and to the conditions of Earth's own natural systems that sustain it -- a real, direct, honest admission that this recognition feels both new and, in hindsight, somewhat obvious. Real, direct closing personal context: recorded during a real, physically limited recovery period following surgery, explicitly offered as a partial explanation for the episode's own unusually raw and intense tone, closing on a direct, personal challenge to the listener rather than a resolution -- asking whether one's own goals are worthy of the single life one gets, since most people only discover otherwise once it's too late to choose again."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct note: this episode was uploaded and built the same day it was published (September 18, 2026), the most recent real episode in this graph as of this build. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Charles Eisenstein's own real 'interbeing' framing and Greg Elliott's own real 'instrumental psychopathic selection hypothesis' are both directly credited and substantively engaged with; both already have existing Persona entries (TGS-232, TGS-231) via their own prior TGS appearances, so no new Human/Persona build was needed here."@en ;
    .

